Cool.

26
00:01:06,220 --> 00:01:10,060
So today, continuing that thread on thinking

27
00:01:10,060 --> 00:01:12,772
about how we can transfer knowledge specifically

28
00:01:12,772 --> 00:01:14,980
within the constraints of these deep learning models,

29
00:01:14,980 --> 00:01:17,420
or these deep learning systems, today, we're

30
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:19,620
going to be specifically talking about how

31
00:01:19,620 --> 00:01:23,340
we can transfer knowledge about the inputs to the system.

32
00:01:23,340 --> 00:01:26,260
And so we're going to spend some time thinking about how

33
00:01:26,260 --> 00:01:28,220
you can think about generative models

34
00:01:28,220 --> 00:01:33,900
as another way to capture information about data inputs.

35
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:37,860
And then we're also going to talk a little bit about models

36
00:01:37,860 --> 00:01:41,260
that learn to learn, so this idea of meta-learning

37
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:44,220
and how you can build models that are almost designed

38
00:01:44,220 --> 00:01:46,460
to be good at transfer learning, designed

39
00:01:46,460 --> 00:01:48,500
to be good at sharing information or learning

40
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:50,460
new things.

41
00:01:50,460 --> 00:01:51,080
Cool.

42
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:57,520
So if we're thinking about knowledge about the inputs,

43
00:01:57,520 --> 00:01:59,120
maybe one way to think about this

44
00:01:59,120 --> 00:02:02,840
is if you know something about the distribution of inputs

45
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:05,240
you're going to expect, maybe there's

46
00:02:05,240 --> 00:02:07,640
a way to actually use that to direct or accelerate

47
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:11,760
your learning or even directly target

48
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:14,840
a specific type of learning.

49
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:21,760
So you have a data set, and then we spent quite a few lectures

50
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:23,880
with Phil talking about this ability

51
00:02:23,880 --> 00:02:28,380
to learn how to generate data from that same distribution,

52
00:02:28,380 --> 00:02:31,080
this idea that you can use generative modeling.

53
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:35,040
And so now, that gives you access

54
00:02:35,040 --> 00:02:37,360
to some model that then is able to sample

55
00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:40,280
from that distribution.

56
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:43,320
And so here, at a first glance, this

57
00:02:43,320 --> 00:02:44,740
might not seem that interesting.

58
00:02:44,740 --> 00:02:46,640
You had data, and then you used it

59
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:49,820
to train a model that mimics the data that you already had.

60
00:02:49,820 --> 00:02:53,440
But you already had the data, so how does it actually help?

61
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:56,420
How are you going to get more information out

62
00:02:56,420 --> 00:02:58,900
of a system that's been trained to model

63
00:02:58,900 --> 00:03:02,500
from a specific distribution?

64
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:05,100
But one of the interesting things

65
00:03:05,100 --> 00:03:08,740
that you can think about this as is,

66
00:03:08,740 --> 00:03:14,460
assuming that generative model is kind of very good,

67
00:03:14,460 --> 00:03:20,000
you can almost think about the model itself as a new way,

68
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:22,500
or a new mechanism to access data,

69
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:26,940
going beyond the capabilities of the initial training data set.

70
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:29,600
So maybe you call it data plus plus.

71
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:32,660
And where does that additive value

72
00:03:32,660 --> 00:03:34,540
that you're getting from a data generator

73
00:03:34,540 --> 00:03:37,860
that you might not get from the data itself?

74
00:03:37,860 --> 00:03:40,420
And here's an interesting quote.

75
00:03:40,420 --> 00:03:44,220
So this is from the release of the first Stable Diffusion

76
00:03:44,220 --> 00:03:44,940
model.

77
00:03:44,940 --> 00:03:48,020
They said that, "This release is the culmination of many hours

78
00:03:48,020 --> 00:03:52,900
of collective effort to create a single file that compresses

79
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:57,600
the visual information of humanity into a few gigabytes."

80
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:00,320
And that sounds pretty awesome.

81
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:03,840
So essentially, it's the idea that, actually,

82
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:09,497
a generative model is a really cool form of data compression.

83
00:04:09,497 --> 00:04:11,080
So you can take that generative model,

84
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:15,380
and you can go from some latent variable to anything on Earth,

85
00:04:15,380 --> 00:04:19,660
like any sort of visual-- all visual information of humanity,

86
00:04:19,660 --> 00:04:23,600
then that is a shocking compression of everything.

87
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:25,880
You think about even just the storage capacity

88
00:04:25,880 --> 00:04:27,480
of all the images on the internet

89
00:04:27,480 --> 00:04:30,320
versus the storage capacity of a Stable Diffusion model.

90
00:04:30,320 --> 00:04:33,000
That's a significant difference in terms

91
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:36,000
of what you're capturing.

92
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:45,440
So here, how do we think about what data plus plus means,

93
00:04:45,440 --> 00:04:50,400
and how do we think about data as maybe like an object?

94
00:04:50,400 --> 00:04:57,140
So if we start with some X, which

95
00:04:57,140 --> 00:05:00,860
is the set of the original training data, which

96
00:05:00,860 --> 00:05:05,580
is little x, z, which is a set of latent variables

97
00:05:05,580 --> 00:05:08,340
that can correspond to the original training data

98
00:05:08,340 --> 00:05:14,580
learned by our generative model, the mapping from z

99
00:05:14,580 --> 00:05:16,620
to x-- so this is basically the mapping that

100
00:05:16,620 --> 00:05:20,920
goes from some latent variable to a generated data point,

101
00:05:20,920 --> 00:05:24,100
and that's G. That's the generative model we've learned.

102
00:05:24,100 --> 00:05:27,380
And then if you also have G inverse-- essentially,

103
00:05:27,380 --> 00:05:30,980
this inverse mapping that lets you go from data to its

104
00:05:30,980 --> 00:05:33,820
associated latent variable--

105
00:05:33,820 --> 00:05:35,980
you can actually define these.

106
00:05:35,980 --> 00:05:38,340
You can take these and define operators

107
00:05:38,340 --> 00:05:41,780
over those objects that enable us to do things

108
00:05:41,780 --> 00:05:46,500
to the generator and the system of data plus generator of data

109
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:49,380
that we couldn't easily do to the data itself.

110
00:05:49,380 --> 00:05:53,480
So for example, you can think about interpolation

111
00:05:53,480 --> 00:05:55,880
over a data set.

112
00:05:55,880 --> 00:05:58,060
Pretty standard definition of interpolation.

113
00:05:58,060 --> 00:05:59,640
But now, you can think about maybe

114
00:05:59,640 --> 00:06:04,520
some-- if you have one set of this, like X,

115
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:09,920
Mathcad X that's some data set, some latent space, a generator

116
00:06:09,920 --> 00:06:12,780
for that latent space to data in the inverse.

117
00:06:12,780 --> 00:06:15,160
If you have one of those, and you have another one that's

118
00:06:15,160 --> 00:06:17,140
for some of different data set, possibly,

119
00:06:17,140 --> 00:06:19,680
or a different generator, you can think about interpolating

120
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:21,400
between the two, right?

121
00:06:21,400 --> 00:06:23,560
So some interpolation between those two things

122
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:26,040
would generate a new one.

123
00:06:26,040 --> 00:06:28,760
And you can think about manipulation, right?

124
00:06:28,760 --> 00:06:32,580
You can think about how you might add some bias term to that

125
00:06:32,580 --> 00:06:36,440
that would give you, again, some whole new kind of object

126
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:39,200
for accessing and interacting with data.

127
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:41,680
You can think about composition.

128
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:44,680
So here, how do you actually kind of compose two things

129
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:46,600
is, of course, different from interpolating

130
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:47,960
between two things.

131
00:06:47,960 --> 00:06:54,060
So now if you essentially are taking this space where

132
00:06:54,060 --> 00:06:58,920
you have just these different data products or data objects,

133
00:06:58,920 --> 00:07:02,860
you can think about really combining them and manipulating

134
00:07:02,860 --> 00:07:04,500
them in interesting ways.

135
00:07:04,500 --> 00:07:07,260
You can also think about optimization,

136
00:07:07,260 --> 00:07:12,940
so how you might actually be able to explicitly find

137
00:07:12,940 --> 00:07:17,980
some set of generator data, latent space inverse generator,

138
00:07:17,980 --> 00:07:22,380
that's optimal for some given dimension of optimality.

139
00:07:22,380 --> 00:07:27,460
And you can imagine taking that and bringing

140
00:07:27,460 --> 00:07:30,405
it to bear on many, many different research

141
00:07:30,405 --> 00:07:32,780
domains or problem domains that people are interested in.

142
00:07:32,780 --> 00:07:35,020
Things like graphics, visualization,

143
00:07:35,020 --> 00:07:39,020
data augmentation, counterfactual reasoning.

144
00:07:39,020 --> 00:07:42,460
And all of this work is kind of somewhat in progress, actually.

145
00:07:42,460 --> 00:07:45,100
So these are just some examples of papers

146
00:07:45,100 --> 00:07:48,460
that start getting at these mechanisms of interpolation,

147
00:07:48,460 --> 00:07:52,480
manipulation, composition, or optimization over generated data

148
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:54,240
objects.

149
00:07:54,240 --> 00:07:59,440
So if we think about this space of generative models--

150
00:07:59,440 --> 00:08:01,320
so here, this is now just visualizing

151
00:08:01,320 --> 00:08:02,653
what we were just talking about.

152
00:08:02,653 --> 00:08:04,480
We have some set of latent variables.

153
00:08:04,480 --> 00:08:06,300
So these are maybe the controls, right?

154
00:08:06,300 --> 00:08:08,640
This is kind of the way that you control

155
00:08:08,640 --> 00:08:10,840
what you want to generate.

156
00:08:10,840 --> 00:08:13,600
And maybe you've made some reasonable constraints

157
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:20,080
in your system so you can ensure that z is normally distributed.

158
00:08:20,080 --> 00:08:25,320
Now, you have some generative model, G,

159
00:08:25,320 --> 00:08:28,960
that will take any point in that latent space

160
00:08:28,960 --> 00:08:30,740
and then synthesize an image.

161
00:08:30,740 --> 00:08:37,280
So here, now, if you sampled a different point in latent space,

162
00:08:37,280 --> 00:08:40,480
then you would sample a different image.

163
00:08:40,480 --> 00:08:44,080
And so now, given the relationship

164
00:08:44,080 --> 00:08:47,860
between dimensionality of movement in latent space

165
00:08:47,860 --> 00:08:49,860
and the image that's generated, you

166
00:08:49,860 --> 00:08:52,940
can think about this as a mechanism of control.

167
00:08:52,940 --> 00:08:56,700
So maybe if you move along some controllable snippet

168
00:08:56,700 --> 00:08:59,180
of the manifold of natural images,

169
00:08:59,180 --> 00:09:03,060
you can explicitly do things like find some latent variable

170
00:09:03,060 --> 00:09:06,100
in your latent space, some dimension in that latent space

171
00:09:06,100 --> 00:09:09,020
that corresponds to something like pose

172
00:09:09,020 --> 00:09:13,760
for this bird or orientation in some way.

173
00:09:13,760 --> 00:09:17,300
And so maybe there's different dimensions

174
00:09:17,300 --> 00:09:20,780
of this control in latent space that corresponds specifically

175
00:09:20,780 --> 00:09:25,220
to disentangled factors of variation or change

176
00:09:25,220 --> 00:09:28,500
in the space of those real generated images.

177
00:09:28,500 --> 00:09:33,280
And so if you can find-- yeah?

178
00:09:33,280 --> 00:09:37,140
AUDIENCE: On that slide, do you suppose that the two dimensions

179
00:09:37,140 --> 00:09:39,740
are linearly independent?

180
00:09:39,740 --> 00:09:41,860
And is it usually the case?

181
00:09:41,860 --> 00:09:44,400
SARA BEERY: So your question is, are you

182
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:48,920
supposing that these dimensions in this kind of latent variable

183
00:09:48,920 --> 00:09:52,120
space are linearly independent?

184
00:09:52,120 --> 00:09:54,080
So I think the assumption here is

185
00:09:54,080 --> 00:10:00,000
that there is some decomposable directions that are maybe

186
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:03,440
some projection of this where you can find orthogonality,

187
00:10:03,440 --> 00:10:05,440
and that orthogonality then corresponds

188
00:10:05,440 --> 00:10:13,920
to some very clean specific variables of variation

189
00:10:13,920 --> 00:10:16,040
in the real image space.

190
00:10:16,040 --> 00:10:24,760
In practice, sometimes, particularly for certain types

191
00:10:24,760 --> 00:10:30,800
or certain categories of generative models, this is true,

192
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:33,380
but it's not necessarily easy to find.

193
00:10:33,380 --> 00:10:38,040
So people will do this cherry picked thing

194
00:10:38,040 --> 00:10:40,200
where they're like, hey, we found a dimension,

195
00:10:40,200 --> 00:10:41,720
where if we move in this dimension,

196
00:10:41,720 --> 00:10:44,640
you can see that it changes from day to night.

197
00:10:44,640 --> 00:10:46,160
We'll talk more about some of these.

198
00:10:46,160 --> 00:10:48,380
But I think one of the challenges of this

199
00:10:48,380 --> 00:10:51,980
is, particularly as these latent spaces get higher dimensional,

200
00:10:51,980 --> 00:10:54,500
being able to really explicitly disentangle

201
00:10:54,500 --> 00:10:56,700
these dimensions of variation in a clean way can

202
00:10:56,700 --> 00:10:59,760
be very difficult and can be highly heuristic.

203
00:10:59,760 --> 00:11:00,260
Yeah.

204
00:11:02,820 --> 00:11:03,620
Cool.

205
00:11:03,620 --> 00:11:07,740
So if you have that, if you have this kind of model that takes

206
00:11:07,740 --> 00:11:11,620
you from data to what we're calling data plus plus,

207
00:11:11,620 --> 00:11:16,860
and you can map now from your actual image now into the latent

208
00:11:16,860 --> 00:11:19,900
space itself-- so this is that G inverse--

209
00:11:19,900 --> 00:11:22,620
now, you have the ability to ask these interesting counterfactual

210
00:11:22,620 --> 00:11:23,340
questions.

211
00:11:23,340 --> 00:11:25,460
What would it look like if assuming

212
00:11:25,460 --> 00:11:28,260
that you have these measurable and actionable dimensions

213
00:11:28,260 --> 00:11:29,940
of variation?

214
00:11:29,940 --> 00:11:32,400
So here, now, you could say, OK.

215
00:11:32,400 --> 00:11:35,580
What would it look like if we moved around

216
00:11:35,580 --> 00:11:36,605
in this latent space?

217
00:11:36,605 --> 00:11:37,980
How do these different dimensions

218
00:11:37,980 --> 00:11:42,160
correspond to different maybe things

219
00:11:42,160 --> 00:11:45,080
within the manifold of real images?

220
00:11:45,080 --> 00:11:47,160
So here's some work where you can actually

221
00:11:47,160 --> 00:11:54,240
show that you can improve your ability to categorize something

222
00:11:54,240 --> 00:11:59,800
by building an ensemble over different generated variations

223
00:11:59,800 --> 00:12:02,320
of that input data in some manifold.

224
00:12:02,320 --> 00:12:04,520
And so you get some, essentially,

225
00:12:04,520 --> 00:12:08,760
improved accuracy and robustness by taking a real input image

226
00:12:08,760 --> 00:12:12,060
and then figuring out where that corresponds to in latent space,

227
00:12:12,060 --> 00:12:15,160
and then moving around nearby in the neighborhood of that thing

228
00:12:15,160 --> 00:12:17,900
to find some maybe different poses, different orientations,

229
00:12:17,900 --> 00:12:20,640
slightly different realistic manipulations of that input

230
00:12:20,640 --> 00:12:23,120
image, and then taking an ensemble.

231
00:12:23,120 --> 00:12:26,840
So it's almost like you're building some robustness

232
00:12:26,840 --> 00:12:30,880
into your categorizer based on some interesting dimensions

233
00:12:30,880 --> 00:12:33,680
of data augmentation, almost like test time

234
00:12:33,680 --> 00:12:38,820
augmentation within the generator itself,

235
00:12:38,820 --> 00:12:39,700
which is interesting.

236
00:12:39,700 --> 00:12:41,200
So there's some nice work that shows

237
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:44,420
that, OK, actually, exploring dimensions of latent space

238
00:12:44,420 --> 00:12:46,940
can give you something that actually

239
00:12:46,940 --> 00:12:49,605
is more than the original training data.

240
00:12:49,605 --> 00:12:51,980
Though I think an interesting and important point here is

241
00:12:51,980 --> 00:12:55,620
there's so much kind of knowledge baked into this.

242
00:12:55,620 --> 00:12:58,980
The fact that something nearby in that image space

243
00:12:58,980 --> 00:13:02,560
should be reasonably-- maybe the same category,

244
00:13:02,560 --> 00:13:04,260
not actually break a category boundary

245
00:13:04,260 --> 00:13:07,380
in some significant way, seems important.

246
00:13:07,380 --> 00:13:09,780
And I often try to point out, some

247
00:13:09,780 --> 00:13:12,260
of these assumptions that are built in, some of the ways

248
00:13:12,260 --> 00:13:13,200
they might fail.

249
00:13:13,200 --> 00:13:17,100
So if you are just trying to understand something like cat,

250
00:13:17,100 --> 00:13:18,475
this is probably very reasonable.

251
00:13:18,475 --> 00:13:20,142
If you're trying to understand something

252
00:13:20,142 --> 00:13:22,660
about maybe a breed of cat, these dimensions of variation

253
00:13:22,660 --> 00:13:24,910
might start to get more confusing in terms of breaking

254
00:13:24,910 --> 00:13:25,840
that class boundary.

255
00:13:25,840 --> 00:13:27,500
Because to me, as someone who spends

256
00:13:27,500 --> 00:13:29,700
a lot of time thinking about animals,

257
00:13:29,700 --> 00:13:32,180
the first image and the one in the middle

258
00:13:32,180 --> 00:13:33,725
actually look like different breeds.

259
00:13:33,725 --> 00:13:35,100
That difference in the morphology

260
00:13:35,100 --> 00:13:37,120
with that extra length of the ears?

261
00:13:37,120 --> 00:13:41,155
To me, if that's this type of cat, the fine grained level

262
00:13:41,155 --> 00:13:42,780
of categorization you're interested in,

263
00:13:42,780 --> 00:13:44,360
this might actually be more confusing

264
00:13:44,360 --> 00:13:46,043
and potentially break that robustness.

265
00:13:46,043 --> 00:13:47,960
So there's always some fundamental assumptions

266
00:13:47,960 --> 00:13:49,335
about what you're actually trying

267
00:13:49,335 --> 00:13:52,640
to do that are built in to the dimensions of variation that

268
00:13:52,640 --> 00:13:57,120
are good or bad for a given problem.

269
00:13:57,120 --> 00:13:58,920
And then there's this question of, how

270
00:13:58,920 --> 00:14:02,283
do you discover these dimensions in a latent space, right?

271
00:14:02,283 --> 00:14:04,200
So one interesting thing you could think about

272
00:14:04,200 --> 00:14:07,175
is, if you're trying to discover an interesting dimension

273
00:14:07,175 --> 00:14:08,800
of variation in a latent space, there's

274
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:10,217
ways you can do it experimentally.

275
00:14:10,217 --> 00:14:12,020
You take an image of something.

276
00:14:12,020 --> 00:14:14,920
You take an image of the same thing at night, for example,

277
00:14:14,920 --> 00:14:17,980
and then you look at where those both mapped to in latent space.

278
00:14:17,980 --> 00:14:20,400
And then you decide, you calculate

279
00:14:20,400 --> 00:14:24,020
the vector of direction that goes from one to the other.

280
00:14:24,020 --> 00:14:26,640
So now, you've almost calculated a vector of direction

281
00:14:26,640 --> 00:14:30,480
that should correspond to this specific type of counterfactual.

282
00:14:30,480 --> 00:14:33,320
And so then you can think about explicitly finding things

283
00:14:33,320 --> 00:14:37,300
like dimensions of variation that zoom out on an image

284
00:14:37,300 --> 00:14:40,580
or zoom in on an image or brighten it or darken it.

285
00:14:40,580 --> 00:14:43,500
So in this case, they were able to find

286
00:14:43,500 --> 00:14:46,420
corresponding specific shifts in the image that

287
00:14:46,420 --> 00:14:50,140
can be used to then control images and ones that

288
00:14:50,140 --> 00:14:51,200
are input agnostic.

289
00:14:51,200 --> 00:14:54,380
So now, you can find this optimal direction or dimension

290
00:14:54,380 --> 00:14:58,260
of variation by just kind of cropping images.

291
00:14:58,260 --> 00:15:00,375
And then you take an ensemble of the directions

292
00:15:00,375 --> 00:15:02,500
for each of those, and maybe you can find something

293
00:15:02,500 --> 00:15:03,440
experimentally.

294
00:15:03,440 --> 00:15:07,160
And so here, you have these dimensions of zoom.

295
00:15:07,160 --> 00:15:09,780
You can find dimensions that correspond to shift even

296
00:15:09,780 --> 00:15:11,700
across different input sizes.

297
00:15:11,700 --> 00:15:13,340
You have dimensions that correspond

298
00:15:13,340 --> 00:15:15,340
to brightening an image.

299
00:15:15,340 --> 00:15:18,020
And then you have things like corresponding dimensions

300
00:15:18,020 --> 00:15:19,440
of darkening an image.

301
00:15:19,440 --> 00:15:21,380
So here, this is interesting, because I

302
00:15:21,380 --> 00:15:23,620
would say that this is really capturing some very

303
00:15:23,620 --> 00:15:25,280
specific bias in our data.

304
00:15:25,280 --> 00:15:29,620
Because clearly, people are not taking pictures of volcanoes

305
00:15:29,620 --> 00:15:31,820
in the dark that aren't erupting.

306
00:15:31,820 --> 00:15:34,640
So when we try to make it dark, it thinks it needs to erupt,

307
00:15:34,640 --> 00:15:35,140
right?

308
00:15:35,140 --> 00:15:36,800
There's this clear bias that's just

309
00:15:36,800 --> 00:15:39,240
built into the training data.

310
00:15:39,240 --> 00:15:40,880
And there are some other cool examples

311
00:15:40,880 --> 00:15:42,940
of this for other types of modalities as well.

312
00:15:42,940 --> 00:15:46,500
So there's actually a really famous example from word2vec,

313
00:15:46,500 --> 00:15:50,520
which is an embedding model for words like semantic meanings,

314
00:15:50,520 --> 00:15:53,720
where there's a direction in the latent

315
00:15:53,720 --> 00:15:57,440
space of models that will correspond to changing

316
00:15:57,440 --> 00:15:58,800
the tense of the word.

317
00:15:58,800 --> 00:16:00,200
So you can move in that direction

318
00:16:00,200 --> 00:16:03,240
and go from swimming to swam and go

319
00:16:03,240 --> 00:16:06,600
from walking to walk by moving the same direction

320
00:16:06,600 --> 00:16:09,360
in that latent space for words.

321
00:16:09,360 --> 00:16:14,520
So these types of examples of disentangled dimensions

322
00:16:14,520 --> 00:16:16,760
of variation in latent space can be discovered

323
00:16:16,760 --> 00:16:18,320
for many modalities and have been

324
00:16:18,320 --> 00:16:19,940
discovered for many modalities.

325
00:16:23,280 --> 00:16:27,840
And then there's kind of like these explicit latent

326
00:16:27,840 --> 00:16:31,580
space vectors that are disentangling

327
00:16:31,580 --> 00:16:32,720
these factors of variation.

328
00:16:32,720 --> 00:16:35,220
So you have things like winter to spring,

329
00:16:35,220 --> 00:16:38,860
or turning on the lights, going from day to night.

330
00:16:38,860 --> 00:16:40,940
The volcano eruption vector.

331
00:16:40,940 --> 00:16:43,260
Though, again, you'll note that you're

332
00:16:43,260 --> 00:16:46,660
more likely to get a more aggressive looking

333
00:16:46,660 --> 00:16:51,060
eruptions at nighttime than during the day.

334
00:16:51,060 --> 00:16:53,580
So there's been a lot of research

335
00:16:53,580 --> 00:16:56,980
into how to efficiently discover these dimensions of variation

336
00:16:56,980 --> 00:16:59,300
within a latent space, these disentangled dimensions

337
00:16:59,300 --> 00:17:02,020
of variation, so let's look at that in a little bit more

338
00:17:02,020 --> 00:17:03,620
technical detail.

339
00:17:03,620 --> 00:17:07,700
So you can take a GAN, like a generative adversarial network,

340
00:17:07,700 --> 00:17:11,859
and you can walk in a straight line in a latent space

341
00:17:11,859 --> 00:17:15,260
and visualize what it looks like to walk in that straight line.

342
00:17:15,260 --> 00:17:17,960
And it kind of works, but it can be really cherry picking.

343
00:17:17,960 --> 00:17:21,099
So some of those directions correspond

344
00:17:21,099 --> 00:17:23,140
to stuff that just looks like trash

345
00:17:23,140 --> 00:17:25,540
or is just not easily disentangled into something

346
00:17:25,540 --> 00:17:27,220
that we can interpret.

347
00:17:27,220 --> 00:17:29,840
And then there's some of fancier versions of a GAN,

348
00:17:29,840 --> 00:17:32,840
like StyleGAN, where they actually explicitly noticed

349
00:17:32,840 --> 00:17:37,680
that any layer of the model, any of those intermediate

350
00:17:37,680 --> 00:17:41,300
representations, also represents a latent space for the model.

351
00:17:41,300 --> 00:17:45,080
And so they determined that there's actually

352
00:17:45,080 --> 00:17:49,020
earlier layers in the model that are better,

353
00:17:49,020 --> 00:17:52,480
quote, unquote, latent representations than

354
00:17:52,480 --> 00:17:55,920
the z representation, that initial input representation.

355
00:17:55,920 --> 00:18:00,060
And so then we'll try and talk about why that might be,

356
00:18:00,060 --> 00:18:02,720
why it might be better to actually think about the latent

357
00:18:02,720 --> 00:18:05,320
space from somewhere intermediate in the model

358
00:18:05,320 --> 00:18:10,160
versus explicitly with that input latent vector.

359
00:18:10,160 --> 00:18:16,040
So if we have some natural image manifold, X,

360
00:18:16,040 --> 00:18:20,920
so some nonlinear data space, and now,

361
00:18:20,920 --> 00:18:25,320
we have some starting point, so this is the corresponding point

362
00:18:25,320 --> 00:18:28,140
in that data natural image manifold corresponding

363
00:18:28,140 --> 00:18:29,900
to this bird, this blue bird.

364
00:18:29,900 --> 00:18:32,580
Maybe you have another point here.

365
00:18:32,580 --> 00:18:34,740
This is corresponding to this fly.

366
00:18:34,740 --> 00:18:37,460
So if you think about linear interpolation between these two

367
00:18:37,460 --> 00:18:43,660
points, arguably, you're moving off the manifold of real images.

368
00:18:43,660 --> 00:18:48,740
There's stuff in one end and in the other that

369
00:18:48,740 --> 00:18:52,860
are more realistic looking, but this direct linear interpolation

370
00:18:52,860 --> 00:18:54,500
in image space just gives you stuff

371
00:18:54,500 --> 00:18:58,060
in the middle that's basically these obvious kind of just

372
00:18:58,060 --> 00:19:00,020
additive images.

373
00:19:00,020 --> 00:19:03,740
And it doesn't look anything like any realistic natural world

374
00:19:03,740 --> 00:19:04,280
image would.

375
00:19:04,280 --> 00:19:05,780
It just doesn't match the statistics

376
00:19:05,780 --> 00:19:07,420
of real natural images.

377
00:19:07,420 --> 00:19:13,100
So instead, if we have some sort of latent space that--

378
00:19:13,100 --> 00:19:15,600
now, we've learned this data plus plus representation.

379
00:19:15,600 --> 00:19:18,840
So now, we have a latent space that's well-behaved,

380
00:19:18,840 --> 00:19:22,900
and we can map from that directly to that natural image

381
00:19:22,900 --> 00:19:24,340
manifold.

382
00:19:24,340 --> 00:19:27,040
Then, you can imagine that interpolating linearly

383
00:19:27,040 --> 00:19:29,920
in the latent space would correspond to a nonlinear

384
00:19:29,920 --> 00:19:32,060
interpolation in that data manifold space,

385
00:19:32,060 --> 00:19:34,320
but it would ensure that you're mapping

386
00:19:34,320 --> 00:19:37,340
within the manifold of real natural images the whole way.

387
00:19:37,340 --> 00:19:40,400
Because by definition, we've constructed something

388
00:19:40,400 --> 00:19:42,200
where any point in that latent space

389
00:19:42,200 --> 00:19:45,680
will map to the natural image manifold.

390
00:19:45,680 --> 00:19:49,200
So now, if you do that same interpolation-- and this is

391
00:19:49,200 --> 00:19:51,840
from a paper called BigGAN back in 2018--

392
00:19:51,840 --> 00:19:55,800
you do get stuff that at least looks a little bit more

393
00:19:55,800 --> 00:19:57,300
like natural images.

394
00:19:57,300 --> 00:19:59,300
But they might not really map to reality.

395
00:19:59,300 --> 00:20:02,780
You get some really weird things like this half bird,

396
00:20:02,780 --> 00:20:04,840
half fly thing where, basically, the model

397
00:20:04,840 --> 00:20:06,080
is just doing its best.

398
00:20:06,080 --> 00:20:08,080
It's doing its best to find some way

399
00:20:08,080 --> 00:20:10,480
to reasonably interpolate between these two things

400
00:20:10,480 --> 00:20:13,280
and stay within the constraints of that trained

401
00:20:13,280 --> 00:20:17,160
natural image manifold mapping.

402
00:20:17,160 --> 00:20:20,255
But that z space, so this z space

403
00:20:20,255 --> 00:20:21,880
here where we're mapping from one point

404
00:20:21,880 --> 00:20:25,940
to another in a linear way, might not

405
00:20:25,940 --> 00:20:30,560
be the best way to organize the data to interpolate along.

406
00:20:30,560 --> 00:20:33,140
And so let's look at why that might be.

407
00:20:33,140 --> 00:20:36,640
So it goes back to the lecture from VAEs.

408
00:20:36,640 --> 00:20:39,460
If you guys remember, you have this data distribution

409
00:20:39,460 --> 00:20:41,275
that maps to a latent distribution,

410
00:20:41,275 --> 00:20:42,900
and you have a latent distribution that

411
00:20:42,900 --> 00:20:44,780
maps to a data distribution.

412
00:20:44,780 --> 00:20:47,500
And so at the bottom, we're coloring the correspondence

413
00:20:47,500 --> 00:20:49,140
between those two spaces, essentially,

414
00:20:49,140 --> 00:20:51,980
like if you're going to map these two things back and forth.

415
00:20:51,980 --> 00:20:57,540
So here, if I say the same color on the natural image manifold

416
00:20:57,540 --> 00:21:00,140
of green, for example, would match the same color green

417
00:21:00,140 --> 00:21:01,860
in the latent space.

418
00:21:01,860 --> 00:21:07,580
So now, let's visualize what happens as this gets trained.

419
00:21:07,580 --> 00:21:10,860
So here, if we're watching this thing get trained,

420
00:21:10,860 --> 00:21:16,180
you can see how it's going from the natural image manifold

421
00:21:16,180 --> 00:21:17,120
to that latent space.

422
00:21:17,120 --> 00:21:19,780
It's almost like you've taken something and tried

423
00:21:19,780 --> 00:21:22,040
to crumple it into a ball.

424
00:21:22,040 --> 00:21:24,480
And so even though we've said explicitly

425
00:21:24,480 --> 00:21:26,600
that, OK, everything in that latent space

426
00:21:26,600 --> 00:21:29,480
should correspond to something on the natural image manifold,

427
00:21:29,480 --> 00:21:32,400
actually, there's this weird stuff here.

428
00:21:32,400 --> 00:21:37,898
There's danger zones where essentially, here, it's

429
00:21:37,898 --> 00:21:39,940
going to be closer to the natural image manifold.

430
00:21:39,940 --> 00:21:41,920
But if you're going from here to here,

431
00:21:41,920 --> 00:21:46,440
there's going to maybe be seams in that linear interpolation

432
00:21:46,440 --> 00:21:52,040
that will correspond to pretty jarring shifts

433
00:21:52,040 --> 00:21:55,000
where things that are nearby in latent space

434
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:57,640
could potentially be quite far away from each other in data

435
00:21:57,640 --> 00:21:59,360
space.

436
00:21:59,360 --> 00:22:01,940
And so essentially, that z representation,

437
00:22:01,940 --> 00:22:05,060
if you're doing linear interpolation across that seam,

438
00:22:05,060 --> 00:22:06,540
it could be unnatural.

439
00:22:06,540 --> 00:22:10,000
And that might be one of the ways

440
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,560
to motivate the discovery that they

441
00:22:12,560 --> 00:22:15,200
found in the StyleGAN paper, which is essentially

442
00:22:15,200 --> 00:22:18,600
that using some intermediate representation somewhere

443
00:22:18,600 --> 00:22:22,180
in between that's not quite fully convex, but maybe

444
00:22:22,180 --> 00:22:25,980
like a little bit less distorted--

445
00:22:25,980 --> 00:22:27,860
so some kind of intermediate thing

446
00:22:27,860 --> 00:22:32,340
where you're possibly less likely to jump across a seam

447
00:22:32,340 --> 00:22:35,820
or fall into some danger zone.

448
00:22:35,820 --> 00:22:37,880
Basically, just somewhere in between

449
00:22:37,880 --> 00:22:40,220
might be the right place to interpolate and get

450
00:22:40,220 --> 00:22:43,740
things that look more realistic and behave

451
00:22:43,740 --> 00:22:46,140
in a more friendly way.

452
00:22:46,140 --> 00:22:48,780
And empirically and qualitatively,

453
00:22:48,780 --> 00:22:51,180
that's sometimes true.

454
00:22:51,180 --> 00:22:53,300
It's our favorite way to say things, right?

455
00:22:53,300 --> 00:22:54,260
Sometimes.

456
00:22:54,260 --> 00:22:56,960
But it does actually correspond in some nice ways.

457
00:22:56,960 --> 00:23:02,200
So here, this was a work where they took that w space,

458
00:23:02,200 --> 00:23:05,740
that intermediate space, and they demonstrated that they

459
00:23:05,740 --> 00:23:09,500
could use it using-- this was a work called StyleSpace--

460
00:23:09,500 --> 00:23:12,350
to very cleanly isolate and disentangle

461
00:23:12,350 --> 00:23:13,600
these dimensions of variation.

462
00:23:13,600 --> 00:23:15,683
And they got some really nice qualitative results.

463
00:23:15,683 --> 00:23:19,040
So here, they're able to vary things

464
00:23:19,040 --> 00:23:23,240
at the level of granularity of hood styles or headlight types

465
00:23:23,240 --> 00:23:26,340
or body colors or background.

466
00:23:29,120 --> 00:23:29,620
Yeah.

467
00:23:29,620 --> 00:23:32,200
So it does seem to suggest that basically

468
00:23:32,200 --> 00:23:37,440
by removing some of those, like weird, badly behaved portions

469
00:23:37,440 --> 00:23:40,400
of linear interpolation, by moving to this more intermediate

470
00:23:40,400 --> 00:23:42,040
space, is beneficial when you're trying

471
00:23:42,040 --> 00:23:45,720
to find these disentangled dimensions of variation.

472
00:23:45,720 --> 00:23:47,040
Cool.

473
00:23:47,040 --> 00:23:51,780
So now, let's talk about how you would label this generated data.

474
00:23:51,780 --> 00:23:55,180
So you have a label for all your real images.

475
00:23:55,180 --> 00:23:57,120
Well, maybe you don't, but assume you do.

476
00:23:57,120 --> 00:23:58,960
Assume that the data you started from,

477
00:23:58,960 --> 00:24:00,877
that you're training this generative model on,

478
00:24:00,877 --> 00:24:02,040
you had labeled.

479
00:24:02,040 --> 00:24:05,800
And now, you have some space in here

480
00:24:05,800 --> 00:24:08,220
that corresponds to that real data.

481
00:24:08,220 --> 00:24:10,000
But then there's a lot of other space

482
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:14,040
that corresponds to purely generated data.

483
00:24:14,040 --> 00:24:16,860
So the argument here is that by construction,

484
00:24:16,860 --> 00:24:20,700
that things that are close together in this manifold,

485
00:24:20,700 --> 00:24:22,880
should be semantically related.

486
00:24:22,880 --> 00:24:26,040
And so if you don't move too far on the manifold,

487
00:24:26,040 --> 00:24:30,220
you should argue that the category should stay the same,

488
00:24:30,220 --> 00:24:32,420
or at least this is the assumption.

489
00:24:32,420 --> 00:24:35,940
And so then you can rely on the normal inductive bias

490
00:24:35,940 --> 00:24:37,940
and machine learning that assigns similar labels

491
00:24:37,940 --> 00:24:40,160
to nearby points, if it's reasonably well

492
00:24:40,160 --> 00:24:41,620
semantically clustered.

493
00:24:41,620 --> 00:24:44,140
So here, if you know, for example, these

494
00:24:44,140 --> 00:24:47,220
are real data points where you know their true label,

495
00:24:47,220 --> 00:24:49,740
then you could assume that things sampled

496
00:24:49,740 --> 00:24:52,180
from those similar data points would actually

497
00:24:52,180 --> 00:24:54,300
correspond to similar things.

498
00:24:54,300 --> 00:24:57,800
And so here, maybe in this region,

499
00:24:57,800 --> 00:25:00,527
yes, there's dimensions of variation that are captured,

500
00:25:00,527 --> 00:25:02,860
but they're not changing the underlying semantic meaning

501
00:25:02,860 --> 00:25:06,100
of the object itself, that these small variations are

502
00:25:06,100 --> 00:25:09,140
more corresponding to these dimensions of variation

503
00:25:09,140 --> 00:25:14,560
in the context, in maybe the scene, the visualization,

504
00:25:14,560 --> 00:25:20,240
but it's not changing the category that's being captured.

505
00:25:20,240 --> 00:25:22,240
And so this was explicitly explored

506
00:25:22,240 --> 00:25:26,640
in this paper called DatasetGAN where they basically

507
00:25:26,640 --> 00:25:29,840
trained a GAN on one type of data,

508
00:25:29,840 --> 00:25:34,760
and then they wanted to solve a new task related to that data,

509
00:25:34,760 --> 00:25:38,400
so specifically labeling the parts of a car

510
00:25:38,400 --> 00:25:41,000
with semantic segmentation.

511
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:43,520
And so then what they did is they used StyleGAN

512
00:25:43,520 --> 00:25:46,120
to efficiently label data in terms

513
00:25:46,120 --> 00:25:48,200
of the semantic segmentation labels, which

514
00:25:48,200 --> 00:25:50,200
can be quite expensive, by defining

515
00:25:50,200 --> 00:25:54,000
an additional labeling arm, which is essentially

516
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,960
the output of a StyleGAN with just a few examples.

517
00:25:56,960 --> 00:26:00,400
And then you can use that model to generate a really large data

518
00:26:00,400 --> 00:26:02,662
set of images and corresponding labels.

519
00:26:02,662 --> 00:26:04,120
They are weak labels, but there are

520
00:26:04,120 --> 00:26:08,740
labels that are explicitly based on this well-structured,

521
00:26:08,740 --> 00:26:11,100
well-aligned latent space.

522
00:26:11,100 --> 00:26:13,100
And then you can train a part segmentation model

523
00:26:13,100 --> 00:26:15,420
on the synthetic data and test it on the real data

524
00:26:15,420 --> 00:26:17,540
and show that it's actually beneficial.

525
00:26:17,540 --> 00:26:21,340
So being a little bit more specific about this,

526
00:26:21,340 --> 00:26:24,940
essentially, if you're trying to train that labeling arm,

527
00:26:24,940 --> 00:26:27,660
they do that using the latent space and then just

528
00:26:27,660 --> 00:26:31,820
a really, really small number of manually labeled examples.

529
00:26:31,820 --> 00:26:33,540
And this is valuable, particularly

530
00:26:33,540 --> 00:26:36,873
because semantic segmentation labels are really expensive.

531
00:26:36,873 --> 00:26:38,540
How many of you have ever actually tried

532
00:26:38,540 --> 00:26:42,100
to label data for semantic segmentation?

533
00:26:42,100 --> 00:26:42,600
Yeah.

534
00:26:42,600 --> 00:26:43,740
It sucks.

535
00:26:43,740 --> 00:26:45,780
It's super slow.

536
00:26:45,780 --> 00:26:48,380
Especially the parts that are really fine

537
00:26:48,380 --> 00:26:50,480
grained, like the boundaries of objects,

538
00:26:50,480 --> 00:26:54,500
it just takes forever to really try to get it very correct.

539
00:26:54,500 --> 00:26:58,780
And so being able to efficiently benefit from just a few of those

540
00:26:58,780 --> 00:27:02,500
and then be able to generalize well is pretty valuable.

541
00:27:02,500 --> 00:27:06,500
So essentially, here, you map human labels in the pixel space

542
00:27:06,500 --> 00:27:09,980
through the network to some of these intermediate features that

543
00:27:09,980 --> 00:27:14,880
have some sort of maintained spatial relationships

544
00:27:14,880 --> 00:27:16,480
and arrangement.

545
00:27:16,480 --> 00:27:20,547
So basically, you take the original data,

546
00:27:20,547 --> 00:27:22,880
and then you run it through what they're calling a style

547
00:27:22,880 --> 00:27:24,255
interpreter, but it's essentially

548
00:27:24,255 --> 00:27:26,000
something like a StyleGAN.

549
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:33,160
That gets you a representation of these specific pixel vectors

550
00:27:33,160 --> 00:27:37,420
that you can then train a model, a really efficient model,

551
00:27:37,420 --> 00:27:40,400
predictive model, to map between those features and part labels.

552
00:27:40,400 --> 00:27:43,080
So essentially, it's like relying on the fact

553
00:27:43,080 --> 00:27:45,320
that training this generator forced the model

554
00:27:45,320 --> 00:27:49,720
to learn a really well-behaved, well-aligned representation

555
00:27:49,720 --> 00:27:52,680
space in terms of these dimensions of variation

556
00:27:52,680 --> 00:27:53,743
and similarity.

557
00:27:53,743 --> 00:27:56,160
And then they could show that you could really efficiently

558
00:27:56,160 --> 00:28:00,480
train these accurate predictive models because the features

559
00:28:00,480 --> 00:28:03,560
themselves were better organized and more semantically meaningful

560
00:28:03,560 --> 00:28:06,120
than the pixels that you might be starting with.

561
00:28:06,120 --> 00:28:11,260
So instead of trying to go from images and pixel labels

562
00:28:11,260 --> 00:28:14,460
to a trained model that generates pixel labels given

563
00:28:14,460 --> 00:28:17,620
this input image, you basically take the input image,

564
00:28:17,620 --> 00:28:19,920
run it through to some intermediate feature space,

565
00:28:19,920 --> 00:28:21,878
and then you've trained this really lightweight

566
00:28:21,878 --> 00:28:24,220
predictive model to go from those features that

567
00:28:24,220 --> 00:28:25,980
are learned by a generative model

568
00:28:25,980 --> 00:28:30,860
to the correct semantic segmentation.

569
00:28:30,860 --> 00:28:33,020
And they had some pretty nice examples

570
00:28:33,020 --> 00:28:34,920
of how they were kind of able to do this.

571
00:28:34,920 --> 00:28:36,920
And qualitatively and quantitatively,

572
00:28:36,920 --> 00:28:39,340
they were able to show that this worked quite well,

573
00:28:39,340 --> 00:28:42,740
and they got this kind of nice result, which

574
00:28:42,740 --> 00:28:48,180
is that a single labeled GAN image was worth about 100

575
00:28:48,180 --> 00:28:51,900
labeled regular images, just in terms of the training efficiency

576
00:28:51,900 --> 00:28:56,140
versus the same amount of accuracy.

577
00:28:56,140 --> 00:29:00,570
And these types of things are increasingly commonly explored,

578
00:29:00,570 --> 00:29:02,820
particularly for these types of labels that are really

579
00:29:02,820 --> 00:29:05,300
expensive to collect.

580
00:29:05,300 --> 00:29:08,800
And interestingly, this question of,

581
00:29:08,800 --> 00:29:11,920
how do you efficiently train these types of things based

582
00:29:11,920 --> 00:29:14,780
on well-learned representation spaces or foundation models,

583
00:29:14,780 --> 00:29:16,882
even ones that are not necessarily generative?

584
00:29:16,882 --> 00:29:18,340
has also been really well explored.

585
00:29:18,340 --> 00:29:19,800
And so these days, if you're trying

586
00:29:19,800 --> 00:29:23,080
to do semantic segmentation, most people

587
00:29:23,080 --> 00:29:26,200
start from something that's called SAM, the Segment Anything

588
00:29:26,200 --> 00:29:28,480
Model, which is a model that relied essentially

589
00:29:28,480 --> 00:29:32,980
on building a really large data set

590
00:29:32,980 --> 00:29:35,740
using some pretty cute hacks for self-supervision.

591
00:29:35,740 --> 00:29:38,960
You take an object, and you use copy paste augmentation

592
00:29:38,960 --> 00:29:41,700
to guarantee that you have that correct mask now

593
00:29:41,700 --> 00:29:44,080
in a bunch of different relationships and orientations.

594
00:29:44,080 --> 00:29:46,600
You use some similar ideas from something

595
00:29:46,600 --> 00:29:50,720
like DINOv2 that really captures fine grained semantic meaning

596
00:29:50,720 --> 00:29:52,680
in those feature spaces, and then you're

597
00:29:52,680 --> 00:29:55,640
able to learn a really generalizable and robust

598
00:29:55,640 --> 00:29:57,700
segmentation model on top of that.

599
00:30:00,920 --> 00:30:01,700
Cool.

600
00:30:01,700 --> 00:30:05,040
So then you can also think about how

601
00:30:05,040 --> 00:30:08,460
these generative models' data plus plus approach

602
00:30:08,460 --> 00:30:12,140
can start to teach you how to explain things or increase

603
00:30:12,140 --> 00:30:15,180
the interpretability of some of these dimensions.

604
00:30:15,180 --> 00:30:18,912
So in standard classification, we take an image,

605
00:30:18,912 --> 00:30:20,620
we run it through a classification model,

606
00:30:20,620 --> 00:30:22,560
and it tries to predict cat.

607
00:30:22,560 --> 00:30:23,460
It's like, OK.

608
00:30:23,460 --> 00:30:26,260
It tries to predict a category.

609
00:30:26,260 --> 00:30:30,460
But now, the question is, can we say something

610
00:30:30,460 --> 00:30:34,900
interesting about why the image was classified as a cat using

611
00:30:34,900 --> 00:30:37,060
this ability and this data plus plus space

612
00:30:37,060 --> 00:30:39,940
to explore counterfactuals?

613
00:30:39,940 --> 00:30:42,147
So here, you take the cat.

614
00:30:42,147 --> 00:30:43,980
You take it from this data space and turn it

615
00:30:43,980 --> 00:30:45,780
into this quote, unquote, data plus plus

616
00:30:45,780 --> 00:30:50,420
space where now you have some maybe encoder, some generator.

617
00:30:50,420 --> 00:30:52,540
So you're mapping through, essentially,

618
00:30:52,540 --> 00:30:56,860
your G inverse and then through your G to get the same data.

619
00:30:56,860 --> 00:30:59,540
But now what you can do is you can

620
00:30:59,540 --> 00:31:06,660
look from this predicted class using-- from the generator,

621
00:31:06,660 --> 00:31:09,280
looking at the classes that are predicted,

622
00:31:09,280 --> 00:31:15,320
you can now explicitly try to perturb in the latent space

623
00:31:15,320 --> 00:31:18,920
to understand how that would cause your prediction to change.

624
00:31:18,920 --> 00:31:22,600
So this gives you some ranking of the changes in the latent

625
00:31:22,600 --> 00:31:24,840
space and manipulations of these latent variables

626
00:31:24,840 --> 00:31:28,320
that maximally lead to the idea of changing a concept.

627
00:31:28,320 --> 00:31:32,680
So it helps you understand what maybe features of this image

628
00:31:32,680 --> 00:31:37,160
are most identifiable as the category of interest.

629
00:31:37,160 --> 00:31:40,720
And so here, this StylEx, it's like almost like StyleSpace

630
00:31:40,720 --> 00:31:42,160
explanation approach.

631
00:31:42,160 --> 00:31:46,000
It tries to find the topK StyleSpace directions, so

632
00:31:46,000 --> 00:31:51,160
these latent space directions of variation, that will most affect

633
00:31:51,160 --> 00:31:53,600
which class the model predicts.

634
00:31:53,600 --> 00:31:57,580
And so here, there's some interesting stuff, right?

635
00:31:57,580 --> 00:32:02,260
You can look at what happens if you open the mouth of the thing,

636
00:32:02,260 --> 00:32:04,980
and it turns out that that is a really strong dimension

637
00:32:04,980 --> 00:32:08,040
of variation for moving the prediction from cat to dog,

638
00:32:08,040 --> 00:32:12,980
probably because dogs tend to pant a lot more than cats do.

639
00:32:12,980 --> 00:32:16,100
So it's a very cute interpolation.

640
00:32:18,700 --> 00:32:23,940
You can also look at changing something

641
00:32:23,940 --> 00:32:26,800
about the size of the pupils relative to the eyes,

642
00:32:26,800 --> 00:32:28,860
and that also shifts the probability

643
00:32:28,860 --> 00:32:30,740
that you have something like a cat.

644
00:32:30,740 --> 00:32:34,840
Or you can think about changing how pointy the ears are,

645
00:32:34,840 --> 00:32:37,340
and that also tends to correspond

646
00:32:37,340 --> 00:32:39,140
from an explainability standpoint

647
00:32:39,140 --> 00:32:42,120
to why the model thinks it's a cat versus something else.

648
00:32:42,120 --> 00:32:46,380
And so this kind of gives us this sense of-- it's

649
00:32:46,380 --> 00:32:50,380
just another mechanism to try to probe the underlying

650
00:32:50,380 --> 00:32:52,680
things that the model has learned or has understood.

651
00:32:52,680 --> 00:32:55,440
And you can imagine that this does tell you, again,

652
00:32:55,440 --> 00:32:58,420
something about the underlying bias in our training data,

653
00:32:58,420 --> 00:33:01,080
because there are many cats that have ears that

654
00:33:01,080 --> 00:33:03,800
are kind of shorter like that.

655
00:33:03,800 --> 00:33:06,820
British shorthairs have short ears just like that.

656
00:33:06,820 --> 00:33:10,040
But more often than not, something

657
00:33:10,040 --> 00:33:11,670
that has long, pointy ears is a cat,

658
00:33:11,670 --> 00:33:13,920
and maybe something with a shorter or folded over ears

659
00:33:13,920 --> 00:33:15,320
might be a dog.

660
00:33:15,320 --> 00:33:17,540
So it's capturing some dimensions of bias,

661
00:33:17,540 --> 00:33:20,933
I think, in the data as well.

662
00:33:20,933 --> 00:33:22,600
And so you can think about this in terms

663
00:33:22,600 --> 00:33:27,680
of how we generate maybe class specific explanations.

664
00:33:27,680 --> 00:33:32,480
So here, if you have something like a perceived age classifier,

665
00:33:32,480 --> 00:33:36,920
then you can start really probing these weird dimensions

666
00:33:36,920 --> 00:33:42,380
of bias and see how that affects the perceived age of a model.

667
00:33:42,380 --> 00:33:47,870
So now, the model is predicting an age.

668
00:33:47,870 --> 00:33:51,040
And so one of the things that's quite odd

669
00:33:51,040 --> 00:33:57,480
here is you'll see specifically that thicker eyebrows are

670
00:33:57,480 --> 00:34:01,580
corresponding responding to more youthful age.

671
00:34:01,580 --> 00:34:05,020
Lighter skin is corresponding to more youthful age, though also

672
00:34:05,020 --> 00:34:09,060
you'll note that it's lighter, but also sometimes less

673
00:34:09,060 --> 00:34:12,120
textured, which is one of these dimensions.

674
00:34:12,120 --> 00:34:14,100
It's not a super, super disentangled dimension

675
00:34:14,100 --> 00:34:15,179
of variation.

676
00:34:15,179 --> 00:34:20,020
Or something like adding glasses or very obviously going

677
00:34:20,020 --> 00:34:22,380
to gray hair.

678
00:34:22,380 --> 00:34:24,370
So these were all things that were learned

679
00:34:24,370 --> 00:34:26,620
in terms of finding these dimensions of variation that

680
00:34:26,620 --> 00:34:29,820
corresponded to specific types of prediction.

681
00:34:29,820 --> 00:34:31,820
And it maybe makes sense.

682
00:34:31,820 --> 00:34:34,760
Older people might be, in terms of your data bias,

683
00:34:34,760 --> 00:34:37,820
more likely to have white hair.

684
00:34:37,820 --> 00:34:41,139
That's something that's actually pretty ubiquitous.

685
00:34:41,139 --> 00:34:43,790
But you could also imagine that some

686
00:34:43,790 --> 00:34:45,540
of these dimensions of variation would not

687
00:34:45,540 --> 00:34:48,940
be something you would want a model to necessarily learn.

688
00:34:48,940 --> 00:34:50,860
These might capture dimensions of bias

689
00:34:50,860 --> 00:34:53,300
that may be related to the fairness of the equity

690
00:34:53,300 --> 00:34:55,739
of a model that we deploy.

691
00:34:55,739 --> 00:34:58,260
So then what do you actually do with what you learn?

692
00:34:58,260 --> 00:35:01,400
Well, one way we could look at this

693
00:35:01,400 --> 00:35:03,840
is like, well, instead of looking

694
00:35:03,840 --> 00:35:05,720
at things like these dimensions of variation

695
00:35:05,720 --> 00:35:08,240
from internet images, can we take it

696
00:35:08,240 --> 00:35:10,920
to scientific data or medical data?

697
00:35:10,920 --> 00:35:15,720
So here, this is using that same type of explainability mechanism

698
00:35:15,720 --> 00:35:19,640
from a generative model to try to understand

699
00:35:19,640 --> 00:35:23,880
how to categorize different retinal fundus images.

700
00:35:23,880 --> 00:35:27,560
And here, we're showing the top four examples of things

701
00:35:27,560 --> 00:35:29,880
that real doctors are looking for

702
00:35:29,880 --> 00:35:34,240
and then how you might actually change that image maximally

703
00:35:34,240 --> 00:35:36,640
to correspond to something predictive

704
00:35:36,640 --> 00:35:39,760
of a specific disease, for example.

705
00:35:39,760 --> 00:35:41,560
And this is quite interesting because it

706
00:35:41,560 --> 00:35:43,620
does actually-- if you show these to a doctor,

707
00:35:43,620 --> 00:35:45,972
it does correspond with the types of even very

708
00:35:45,972 --> 00:35:47,680
fine grained features that a doctor might

709
00:35:47,680 --> 00:35:50,520
use to actually categorize a specific type of disease.

710
00:35:50,520 --> 00:35:53,260
So from that sense, from an explainability standpoint,

711
00:35:53,260 --> 00:35:55,060
it's almost a way to build trust in a model

712
00:35:55,060 --> 00:35:56,760
because an expert would say, OK, yeah.

713
00:35:56,760 --> 00:35:57,680
No, I do agree.

714
00:35:57,680 --> 00:36:01,420
I do agree that is a reasonable dimension of variation

715
00:36:01,420 --> 00:36:05,660
that I also would correspond to a specific type of disease.

716
00:36:05,660 --> 00:36:08,420
It's almost a reassurance that the model isn't picking up

717
00:36:08,420 --> 00:36:11,220
too many odd other correlative factors that

718
00:36:11,220 --> 00:36:15,220
might be things that we don't want to model to correspond to.

719
00:36:15,220 --> 00:36:17,220
But of course, one of the really big limitations

720
00:36:17,220 --> 00:36:20,180
of all of these approaches is that you are

721
00:36:20,180 --> 00:36:22,880
required to figure out how--

722
00:36:22,880 --> 00:36:25,660
you have to discover those latent space variations

723
00:36:25,660 --> 00:36:27,540
and then manually analyze or to find

724
00:36:27,540 --> 00:36:29,260
what they might correspond to.

725
00:36:29,260 --> 00:36:31,520
And that takes a lot of effort, right?

726
00:36:31,520 --> 00:36:33,180
So getting to the point where they

727
00:36:33,180 --> 00:36:36,860
had these examples of these specific dimensions of variation

728
00:36:36,860 --> 00:36:41,420
that corresponded really well to these real diseases,

729
00:36:41,420 --> 00:36:45,900
I'm sure it took a lot of handcrafting and a lot of time.

730
00:36:45,900 --> 00:36:49,540
And so recent work tries to do similar types

731
00:36:49,540 --> 00:36:52,780
of counterfactual reasoning or understanding.

732
00:36:52,780 --> 00:36:55,360
But here, I'm actually just using

733
00:36:55,360 --> 00:36:58,120
text conditioning for modern diffusion models,

734
00:36:58,120 --> 00:37:00,880
or other types of conditioning using diffusion models, that

735
00:37:00,880 --> 00:37:03,360
give us really simple and interpretable mechanisms

736
00:37:03,360 --> 00:37:06,600
for control, though, of course, there's not always perfect

737
00:37:06,600 --> 00:37:08,840
alignment between maybe your text control

738
00:37:08,840 --> 00:37:12,400
and what actually gets generated by the generator.

739
00:37:12,400 --> 00:37:14,320
But it does make this ability to test

740
00:37:14,320 --> 00:37:18,900
these counterfactual hypotheses, in many ways, pretty efficient.

741
00:37:18,900 --> 00:37:22,000
So I showed this work actually before,

742
00:37:22,000 --> 00:37:24,680
but this is work where they very specifically looked

743
00:37:24,680 --> 00:37:28,200
at using almost like human intelligence

744
00:37:28,200 --> 00:37:30,140
to generate possible counterfactuals,

745
00:37:30,140 --> 00:37:32,480
using text conditioning on generative images,

746
00:37:32,480 --> 00:37:36,640
and then explicitly testing, quantitatively, the performance

747
00:37:36,640 --> 00:37:41,480
of models given these different human-derived counterfactuals.

748
00:37:41,480 --> 00:37:45,480
And they showed that it's just a really nice way to do,

749
00:37:45,480 --> 00:37:48,400
essentially, interfaces with your data sets,

750
00:37:48,400 --> 00:37:52,210
basically using the generator trained on top of data

751
00:37:52,210 --> 00:37:57,810
as a direct investigation mechanism or interaction

752
00:37:57,810 --> 00:38:00,170
mechanism with the underlying real data

753
00:38:00,170 --> 00:38:02,950
set itself understanding the dimensions of bias.

754
00:38:05,530 --> 00:38:07,490
Cool.

755
00:38:07,490 --> 00:38:09,810
And then I think there's also this question of--

756
00:38:09,810 --> 00:38:11,430
OK.

757
00:38:11,430 --> 00:38:14,210
The data the generated data is potentially

758
00:38:14,210 --> 00:38:18,430
useful for explainability or for our data set exploration.

759
00:38:18,430 --> 00:38:22,770
But it is also useful for representation learning?

760
00:38:22,770 --> 00:38:26,547
Can you learn from generated data in the same way

761
00:38:26,547 --> 00:38:29,130
that you might be able to learn, or maybe even in a better way

762
00:38:29,130 --> 00:38:31,490
than you might be able to learn, from real data?

763
00:38:31,490 --> 00:38:34,410
And this is where it gets kind of complicated.

764
00:38:34,410 --> 00:38:36,930
So here's a work where they basically said, OK, you

765
00:38:36,930 --> 00:38:40,810
have some data set x, and then you're

766
00:38:40,810 --> 00:38:44,730
going to train a generative model on top of that data set.

767
00:38:44,730 --> 00:38:47,675
And the idea is somehow the generative model

768
00:38:47,675 --> 00:38:49,050
trained on top of the data set is

769
00:38:49,050 --> 00:38:53,010
going to capture either the same information as the data set,

770
00:38:53,010 --> 00:38:55,030
or maybe arguably slightly more information

771
00:38:55,030 --> 00:38:57,430
than the underlying data set.

772
00:38:57,430 --> 00:38:59,230
And then that might actually be able to be

773
00:38:59,230 --> 00:39:02,050
useful for representation learning.

774
00:39:02,050 --> 00:39:04,230
So here, kind of similar to that other example

775
00:39:04,230 --> 00:39:06,570
with the multi-view ensembling from GANs,

776
00:39:06,570 --> 00:39:08,510
they're explicitly looking at your ability

777
00:39:08,510 --> 00:39:10,670
to generate maybe the same real image

778
00:39:10,670 --> 00:39:14,590
from different perspectives or from different views,

779
00:39:14,590 --> 00:39:20,030
and then use that as the input to contrastive learning models.

780
00:39:20,030 --> 00:39:22,590
So here, trying to learn a representation space

781
00:39:22,590 --> 00:39:24,750
where you're saying, all right.

782
00:39:24,750 --> 00:39:27,628
Here's two images from the same category.

783
00:39:27,628 --> 00:39:29,170
Here's one from a different category.

784
00:39:29,170 --> 00:39:32,910
And then you're training that contrastive style loss.

785
00:39:32,910 --> 00:39:33,590
Yeah.

786
00:39:33,590 --> 00:39:35,710
AUDIENCE: So is this trained from scratch,

787
00:39:35,710 --> 00:39:37,622
or is this transfer learning to some degree?

788
00:39:37,622 --> 00:39:38,330
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

789
00:39:38,330 --> 00:39:41,070
That's an interesting way-- it's an interesting question.

790
00:39:41,070 --> 00:39:45,230
I mean, arguably, this is a mechanism of transfer learning,

791
00:39:45,230 --> 00:39:46,262
assuming that--

792
00:39:46,262 --> 00:39:48,470
AUDIENCE: Well, I guess to get to the original model,

793
00:39:48,470 --> 00:39:51,130
was that built on some pretrained model

794
00:39:51,130 --> 00:39:52,795
for image analysis?

795
00:39:52,795 --> 00:39:54,170
SARA BEERY: Which original model?

796
00:39:54,170 --> 00:39:57,030
We're talking about kind of a system here.

797
00:39:57,030 --> 00:39:58,370
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

798
00:39:58,370 --> 00:40:00,370
SARA BEERY: This generative model?

799
00:40:00,370 --> 00:40:04,650
So let's assume for the sake of simplicity

800
00:40:04,650 --> 00:40:06,690
that this is trained from scratch on that,

801
00:40:06,690 --> 00:40:09,710
because otherwise, it gets even more complicated to understand.

802
00:40:09,710 --> 00:40:13,170
And this actually, I think, is one of the difficulties

803
00:40:13,170 --> 00:40:17,090
currently when people are trying to understand whether we can--

804
00:40:17,090 --> 00:40:19,970
there's this kind of broader meta argument going

805
00:40:19,970 --> 00:40:21,930
on in the community of whether it

806
00:40:21,930 --> 00:40:25,450
is possible to get more information out

807
00:40:25,450 --> 00:40:30,250
of a generator trained on data than the underlying data itself.

808
00:40:30,250 --> 00:40:31,910
From an information theory perspective,

809
00:40:31,910 --> 00:40:34,010
it's like, how could you possibly create something

810
00:40:34,010 --> 00:40:34,770
from nothing?

811
00:40:34,770 --> 00:40:36,890
If you have the data, and that's all the information you have,

812
00:40:36,890 --> 00:40:39,030
you couldn't maybe get more information out of it.

813
00:40:39,030 --> 00:40:41,210
But then there's kind of the counterargument, which

814
00:40:41,210 --> 00:40:43,730
is actually like our architecture design,

815
00:40:43,730 --> 00:40:47,230
and the design of the training systems

816
00:40:47,230 --> 00:40:49,750
builds in knowledge that would not necessarily

817
00:40:49,750 --> 00:40:51,570
have already been built in.

818
00:40:51,570 --> 00:40:54,350
And actually, you could argue that the construction of things

819
00:40:54,350 --> 00:40:56,390
like convolutional neural networks

820
00:40:56,390 --> 00:40:59,630
fundamentally are an injection of additional knowledge

821
00:40:59,630 --> 00:41:03,470
beyond just the underlying intermediate data.

822
00:41:03,470 --> 00:41:06,950
We're building knowledge into the design of our architectures

823
00:41:06,950 --> 00:41:10,030
and our loss functions based on our understanding of what,

824
00:41:10,030 --> 00:41:12,790
for example, structure we expect to see.

825
00:41:12,790 --> 00:41:15,270
So convolutional neural networks basically say,

826
00:41:15,270 --> 00:41:18,910
we expect there to be local structure in images.

827
00:41:18,910 --> 00:41:23,870
And so then the argument is, isn't data augmentation

828
00:41:23,870 --> 00:41:28,350
a mechanism-- you could argue that random flipping is an image

829
00:41:28,350 --> 00:41:31,310
generation algorithm, right?

830
00:41:31,310 --> 00:41:33,510
I mean, depending on how you want

831
00:41:33,510 --> 00:41:36,250
to define that, semantically, it is, right?

832
00:41:36,250 --> 00:41:37,590
You've taken an image.

833
00:41:37,590 --> 00:41:42,190
You've used some algorithm to generate a new image that you

834
00:41:42,190 --> 00:41:45,870
guarantee still has fidelity to the class that you care about.

835
00:41:49,810 --> 00:41:55,930
And then things like this copy paste, cut and paste

836
00:41:55,930 --> 00:41:57,890
style data augmentation, that's even

837
00:41:57,890 --> 00:41:59,370
getting maybe closer to something

838
00:41:59,370 --> 00:42:01,537
like a learned generative model, because now, you're

839
00:42:01,537 --> 00:42:04,650
building explicit assumptions about taking foreground objects

840
00:42:04,650 --> 00:42:06,630
and putting them on different backgrounds.

841
00:42:06,630 --> 00:42:08,570
This is like a really useful mechanism

842
00:42:08,570 --> 00:42:11,290
for increasing the robustness of machine learning models, just

843
00:42:11,290 --> 00:42:14,290
these engineering hacks about how we manipulate

844
00:42:14,290 --> 00:42:15,663
the data during training.

845
00:42:15,663 --> 00:42:17,330
And then maybe the argument is like, how

846
00:42:17,330 --> 00:42:18,970
is that any different from something

847
00:42:18,970 --> 00:42:21,930
like learning a generator that can interpolate maybe

848
00:42:21,930 --> 00:42:25,090
in something closer to the image manifold?

849
00:42:25,090 --> 00:42:27,830
So it's a bit semantic.

850
00:42:31,250 --> 00:42:33,270
Anyway, I have been thinking about this a lot.

851
00:42:33,270 --> 00:42:35,770
I have a grad student who's been looking at whether we can--

852
00:42:35,770 --> 00:42:37,210
I really, really specifically want

853
00:42:37,210 --> 00:42:40,850
to be able to learn, maybe using generative models,

854
00:42:40,850 --> 00:42:43,710
to do a better job, a more robust job, of recognizing

855
00:42:43,710 --> 00:42:45,430
rare things.

856
00:42:45,430 --> 00:42:47,350
But now, you've really gotten yourself

857
00:42:47,350 --> 00:42:50,090
in a chicken and egg problem.

858
00:42:50,090 --> 00:42:53,590
Because the thing is rare, it's hard to train a generator that

859
00:42:53,590 --> 00:42:55,410
does a good job of generating it,

860
00:42:55,410 --> 00:42:57,325
and then the reverse is also true.

861
00:42:57,325 --> 00:42:59,950
And there have been people that have shown that you can improve

862
00:42:59,950 --> 00:43:02,463
rare categorization using generative models,

863
00:43:02,463 --> 00:43:04,630
but they cheat, and they train the generative models

864
00:43:04,630 --> 00:43:07,220
on a lot of data.

865
00:43:07,220 --> 00:43:10,070
So far, we have been able to find ways

866
00:43:10,070 --> 00:43:12,750
where you can get some gains in very specific constrained

867
00:43:12,750 --> 00:43:15,990
scenarios, but it's not just naive and simple.

868
00:43:15,990 --> 00:43:18,750
Anyway, the point here is that you

869
00:43:18,750 --> 00:43:22,110
can take the classical contrastive learning approach

870
00:43:22,110 --> 00:43:24,750
where we're using our knowledge-based image

871
00:43:24,750 --> 00:43:28,030
generation, where we've done these specific types of warping

872
00:43:28,030 --> 00:43:32,670
or whatever, random cropping, flipping, to the initial image

873
00:43:32,670 --> 00:43:35,510
color jitter, in a way that we know doesn't necessarily

874
00:43:35,510 --> 00:43:36,810
break the category boundary.

875
00:43:36,810 --> 00:43:39,430
We still want it to be the same thing.

876
00:43:39,430 --> 00:43:42,530
And here, we're instead going to move just a little bit

877
00:43:42,530 --> 00:43:48,130
in some latent space to create now this mapping in data space

878
00:43:48,130 --> 00:43:50,850
through what should be the manifold of real images,

879
00:43:50,850 --> 00:43:53,210
and then use that as your transformation

880
00:43:53,210 --> 00:43:55,330
for your contrastive learning model.

881
00:43:55,330 --> 00:43:55,910
Yeah.

882
00:43:55,910 --> 00:43:58,618
AUDIENCE: I had a question about this, which kind of relates back

883
00:43:58,618 --> 00:44:03,570
to earlier when we talked about the idea of small local movement

884
00:44:03,570 --> 00:44:06,530
in the latent space not crossing a boundary.

885
00:44:06,530 --> 00:44:12,050
I mean, intuitively, if you have just a 2D latent space and a 10

886
00:44:12,050 --> 00:44:16,330
class classification problem, you could imagine some very

887
00:44:16,330 --> 00:44:18,690
naive pie chart that has 10 slices.

888
00:44:18,690 --> 00:44:21,170
And there has to exist a boundary

889
00:44:21,170 --> 00:44:23,583
somewhere between category 1 and category 2.

890
00:44:23,583 --> 00:44:24,250
SARA BEERY: Yes.

891
00:44:24,250 --> 00:44:26,333
AUDIENCE: So is this just a probabilistic argument

892
00:44:26,333 --> 00:44:29,090
that in a high enough dimensional latent space,

893
00:44:29,090 --> 00:44:33,130
the odds that you would sample a location where the boundary

894
00:44:33,130 --> 00:44:37,010
shifts is so unlikely that you can get away

895
00:44:37,010 --> 00:44:40,390
with this kind of approach to training for contrastive

896
00:44:40,390 --> 00:44:41,430
learning?

897
00:44:41,430 --> 00:44:43,690
SARA BEERY: Or I would even go beyond that,

898
00:44:43,690 --> 00:44:47,630
and I would even say as long as you define

899
00:44:47,630 --> 00:44:50,510
some dimension of movement--

900
00:44:50,510 --> 00:44:52,550
as long as, most of the time, it's

901
00:44:52,550 --> 00:44:55,270
not crossing a class boundary-- if it does cross a class

902
00:44:55,270 --> 00:44:57,050
boundary a very small amount of the time

903
00:44:57,050 --> 00:44:59,150
still, by the way we train these models,

904
00:44:59,150 --> 00:45:01,210
it's almost like the noise will come out.

905
00:45:01,210 --> 00:45:05,070
It's almost a mechanism for regularization.

906
00:45:05,070 --> 00:45:08,510
Of course, there's counterarguments to that.

907
00:45:08,510 --> 00:45:10,870
It depends on how egregious those boundaries are

908
00:45:10,870 --> 00:45:13,870
and how frequently you're crossing them.

909
00:45:13,870 --> 00:45:14,370
Yeah.

910
00:45:14,370 --> 00:45:17,230
I would say as long as--

911
00:45:17,230 --> 00:45:19,910
if you want to make a probabilistic argument,

912
00:45:19,910 --> 00:45:21,790
and you want to map that to something that's

913
00:45:21,790 --> 00:45:25,950
like a physical area argument, like the area of the model where

914
00:45:25,950 --> 00:45:29,470
moving in a small amount corresponds to a reasonably

915
00:45:29,470 --> 00:45:31,750
maintaining the category boundary

916
00:45:31,750 --> 00:45:34,190
relative to the areas where you are crossing category

917
00:45:34,190 --> 00:45:38,030
boundaries-- as long as that ratio is quite large,

918
00:45:38,030 --> 00:45:40,710
then I imagine you would still learned something useful,

919
00:45:40,710 --> 00:45:41,950
even if sometimes it's wrong.

920
00:45:41,950 --> 00:45:45,370
It's just like how in the standard contrastive learning

921
00:45:45,370 --> 00:45:47,930
objectives, we did see some improvement when

922
00:45:47,930 --> 00:45:51,550
you could explicitly remove these false positive,

923
00:45:51,550 --> 00:45:54,090
false negative type things where, within your batch,

924
00:45:54,090 --> 00:45:56,490
you would have maybe two images of dogs.

925
00:45:56,490 --> 00:45:58,870
And now, they're being treated as a different category

926
00:45:58,870 --> 00:46:00,370
during your representation learning.

927
00:46:00,370 --> 00:46:02,203
You're explicitly saying those things should

928
00:46:02,203 --> 00:46:04,610
be far apart, though they actually

929
00:46:04,610 --> 00:46:08,770
represent the same category.

930
00:46:08,770 --> 00:46:10,610
If you could remove all of those,

931
00:46:10,610 --> 00:46:12,810
the models did train better.

932
00:46:12,810 --> 00:46:14,530
There were these nice examples where

933
00:46:14,530 --> 00:46:16,238
you could show, if you removed these kind

934
00:46:16,238 --> 00:46:19,910
of false characterizations by using supervision, for example,

935
00:46:19,910 --> 00:46:21,630
you got better representation learning.

936
00:46:21,630 --> 00:46:24,130
But the model's still able to learn useful representations

937
00:46:24,130 --> 00:46:26,970
even when those do exist in there.

938
00:46:26,970 --> 00:46:29,610
Yeah.

939
00:46:29,610 --> 00:46:30,110
Cool.

940
00:46:30,110 --> 00:46:30,670
So yeah.

941
00:46:30,670 --> 00:46:32,420
Now, here, it's just a different mechanism

942
00:46:32,420 --> 00:46:34,050
for constructing these positive pairs

943
00:46:34,050 --> 00:46:36,590
to train your contrastive learning algorithm.

944
00:46:36,590 --> 00:46:40,950
And so here, as opposed to your standard SimCLR type views

945
00:46:40,950 --> 00:46:44,310
where you have two views of the same thing where it's maybe

946
00:46:44,310 --> 00:46:48,150
cropped from different areas or warped in terms of the color,

947
00:46:48,150 --> 00:46:50,150
now, you're taking latent views.

948
00:46:50,150 --> 00:46:53,930
You're taking some ball around the real image.

949
00:46:53,930 --> 00:46:55,890
And any dimension, you would move in that ball,

950
00:46:55,890 --> 00:46:58,190
you could argue, would be a different view

951
00:46:58,190 --> 00:47:00,950
of the same thing.

952
00:47:00,950 --> 00:47:05,375
And then now, just how we might say,

953
00:47:05,375 --> 00:47:06,750
OK, these are all different views

954
00:47:06,750 --> 00:47:08,167
of the same thing for the purposes

955
00:47:08,167 --> 00:47:09,510
of contrastive learning.

956
00:47:09,510 --> 00:47:11,510
Here, you can say, these are all different views

957
00:47:11,510 --> 00:47:12,710
of the same thing.

958
00:47:12,710 --> 00:47:16,590
Now, one interesting dimension here, just generally,

959
00:47:16,590 --> 00:47:20,710
is this question of diversity versus fidelity

960
00:47:20,710 --> 00:47:23,600
when it comes to the usefulness of training signal.

961
00:47:23,600 --> 00:47:25,850
This is also something I've been thinking about a lot.

962
00:47:25,850 --> 00:47:29,870
So it's probably more useful to know

963
00:47:29,870 --> 00:47:32,870
that two things that are more different from each other

964
00:47:32,870 --> 00:47:36,050
are, in fact, the same than it is to know that two things that

965
00:47:36,050 --> 00:47:38,970
are almost identical to each other are, in fact, the same.

966
00:47:38,970 --> 00:47:42,450
So for example, if you look at this middle set

967
00:47:42,450 --> 00:47:45,570
here, where these are all American robins,

968
00:47:45,570 --> 00:47:48,410
the pose of this American robin in basically all of these

969
00:47:48,410 --> 00:47:49,350
is nearly identical.

970
00:47:49,350 --> 00:47:52,270
The construction of the image, the orientation,

971
00:47:52,270 --> 00:47:54,410
et cetera, of the image is almost identical.

972
00:47:54,410 --> 00:47:56,690
There's not a lot of diversity here.

973
00:47:56,690 --> 00:47:59,170
And so actually from a learning signal perspective,

974
00:47:59,170 --> 00:48:02,370
this might be less useful, arguably,

975
00:48:02,370 --> 00:48:06,150
than something that also does something like random flipping.

976
00:48:06,150 --> 00:48:08,730
And so I think there is this kind

977
00:48:08,730 --> 00:48:11,710
of interesting challenge with these generative models,

978
00:48:11,710 --> 00:48:15,330
broadly, with GANs, with diffusion models, et cetera.

979
00:48:15,330 --> 00:48:17,930
Even with some of these personalization style

980
00:48:17,930 --> 00:48:21,290
models that are trying to do a really nice job of capturing

981
00:48:21,290 --> 00:48:24,770
something specific, often, there's

982
00:48:24,770 --> 00:48:27,130
almost like a Pareto frontier with these models

983
00:48:27,130 --> 00:48:30,010
when it comes to this trade off between diversity and fidelity.

984
00:48:30,010 --> 00:48:32,910
And so you can't to get the model

985
00:48:32,910 --> 00:48:35,350
to generate things that are really diverse, maybe

986
00:48:35,350 --> 00:48:39,830
in terms of their context, or their scene structure,

987
00:48:39,830 --> 00:48:41,810
without giving up fidelity.

988
00:48:41,810 --> 00:48:46,990
You end up in this space where you can generate a robin that's

989
00:48:46,990 --> 00:48:48,650
positioned differently in the scene,

990
00:48:48,650 --> 00:48:52,030
maybe flying, different pose, and you can try

991
00:48:52,030 --> 00:48:53,370
to force the model to do that.

992
00:48:53,370 --> 00:48:55,350
And it will, but it probably won't actually

993
00:48:55,350 --> 00:48:58,830
be very robin-like anymore if this is the input

994
00:48:58,830 --> 00:49:01,990
image that you're working from.

995
00:49:01,990 --> 00:49:03,150
Yeah.

996
00:49:03,150 --> 00:49:05,270
AUDIENCE: In the latent space, when

997
00:49:05,270 --> 00:49:08,970
you're moving around the image feature vector,

998
00:49:08,970 --> 00:49:11,870
would you say that the images around it

999
00:49:11,870 --> 00:49:14,430
belong to the same class?

1000
00:49:14,430 --> 00:49:16,230
How do you know how far you can go?

1001
00:49:16,230 --> 00:49:20,110
And that distance, is it the same for all classes?

1002
00:49:20,110 --> 00:49:22,430
You can imagine for classes of one,

1003
00:49:22,430 --> 00:49:25,942
one class is overrepresented compared to another class.

1004
00:49:25,942 --> 00:49:26,650
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

1005
00:49:26,650 --> 00:49:28,030
So the question is basically, how

1006
00:49:28,030 --> 00:49:31,290
do you know how far you can go in the latent space

1007
00:49:31,290 --> 00:49:33,850
and have it still map to the same category?

1008
00:49:33,850 --> 00:49:36,810
And does that distance correspond--

1009
00:49:36,810 --> 00:49:38,890
is the optimal amount of distance

1010
00:49:38,890 --> 00:49:40,570
you could go for any given category

1011
00:49:40,570 --> 00:49:42,450
the same across all categories?

1012
00:49:42,450 --> 00:49:46,010
So I would say experimentally, in a lot of these papers,

1013
00:49:46,010 --> 00:49:48,310
they're using this as a hyperparameter, right?

1014
00:49:48,310 --> 00:49:49,810
They're basically saying, all right.

1015
00:49:49,810 --> 00:49:53,410
We're going to define some radius in something

1016
00:49:53,410 --> 00:49:57,890
like cosine distance, and we're going to sample

1017
00:49:57,890 --> 00:49:59,070
within that radius.

1018
00:49:59,070 --> 00:50:02,382
And we're going to test for different radiuses or radii.

1019
00:50:02,382 --> 00:50:04,090
And then we're going to pick the one that

1020
00:50:04,090 --> 00:50:07,570
does the best on our test data.

1021
00:50:07,570 --> 00:50:09,718
That's a bit disappointing, because I actually

1022
00:50:09,718 --> 00:50:11,510
think that's a really interesting question.

1023
00:50:11,510 --> 00:50:16,410
How do you determine how far you can go before it crosses a class

1024
00:50:16,410 --> 00:50:17,150
boundary?

1025
00:50:17,150 --> 00:50:21,050
And almost by definition, you start

1026
00:50:21,050 --> 00:50:22,990
getting into these really messy questions,

1027
00:50:22,990 --> 00:50:25,410
particularly as these models are able to generate

1028
00:50:25,410 --> 00:50:29,610
things that are realistic looking but impossible.

1029
00:50:32,510 --> 00:50:34,710
Me and Phil actually argue about this quite a lot.

1030
00:50:34,710 --> 00:50:37,470
If you had a picture of your mom,

1031
00:50:37,470 --> 00:50:40,050
but you had another eye in the middle of her forehead,

1032
00:50:40,050 --> 00:50:42,670
is it still your mom?

1033
00:50:42,670 --> 00:50:44,890
What do you guys think?

1034
00:50:44,890 --> 00:50:46,770
Is it still your mom if she has three eyes?

1035
00:50:50,550 --> 00:50:51,970
There's not a right answer here.

1036
00:50:51,970 --> 00:50:55,070
And so if some of the dimensions of interpolation

1037
00:50:55,070 --> 00:50:57,693
you can move into are still kind of realistic looking,

1038
00:50:57,693 --> 00:51:00,110
but actually impossible-- so maybe if you think about this

1039
00:51:00,110 --> 00:51:02,130
from a species categorization perspective,

1040
00:51:02,130 --> 00:51:04,010
you take like this American robin,

1041
00:51:04,010 --> 00:51:09,190
and then you make the chest gray instead of red.

1042
00:51:09,190 --> 00:51:11,150
That's not a real bird.

1043
00:51:11,150 --> 00:51:14,893
So now, you have things that look like birds,

1044
00:51:14,893 --> 00:51:16,310
but they don't actually correspond

1045
00:51:16,310 --> 00:51:19,030
to any real category of bird.

1046
00:51:19,030 --> 00:51:21,210
And so then what should that be categorized as?

1047
00:51:21,210 --> 00:51:24,190
And so somehow, understanding the boundary

1048
00:51:24,190 --> 00:51:27,850
of a class, a really important component to being able to do

1049
00:51:27,850 --> 00:51:31,850
that is having representative data from all the dimensions

1050
00:51:31,850 --> 00:51:34,330
of real variation for that class so you

1051
00:51:34,330 --> 00:51:36,590
can define what the reasonable class boundary is.

1052
00:51:36,590 --> 00:51:39,250
That's one of the reasons that few shot learning is so hard,

1053
00:51:39,250 --> 00:51:41,930
because it often means we don't have

1054
00:51:41,930 --> 00:51:43,770
a good, well understood representation

1055
00:51:43,770 --> 00:51:46,150
space of the boundaries of the real class.

1056
00:51:46,150 --> 00:51:47,450
And so we don't know.

1057
00:51:47,450 --> 00:51:50,530
If you've never seen a picture of a bird as a juvenile,

1058
00:51:50,530 --> 00:51:54,790
we don't know what the real class boundary should be.

1059
00:51:54,790 --> 00:51:55,290
Yeah.

1060
00:51:55,290 --> 00:51:57,623
AUDIENCE: Isn't that just like a fundamental shortcoming

1061
00:51:57,623 --> 00:52:00,650
of classification as a learning objective?

1062
00:52:00,650 --> 00:52:04,810
CLIP was almost a better idea where they're just

1063
00:52:04,810 --> 00:52:08,050
saying that you should learn to embed text

1064
00:52:08,050 --> 00:52:11,970
descriptions of images close to that embedding of the image,

1065
00:52:11,970 --> 00:52:15,490
and you can capture more truth about the world.

1066
00:52:15,490 --> 00:52:17,068
To your point about the robin chest--

1067
00:52:17,068 --> 00:52:18,110
I mean, this isn't exact.

1068
00:52:18,110 --> 00:52:21,810
But imagine you have an albino robin.

1069
00:52:21,810 --> 00:52:24,330
That's still a robin, and a human can look at it

1070
00:52:24,330 --> 00:52:25,850
and be like, yeah, that's a robin.

1071
00:52:25,850 --> 00:52:29,670
But from your definition of the classification perspective,

1072
00:52:29,670 --> 00:52:30,190
I doubt--

1073
00:52:30,190 --> 00:52:34,050
I think you're right that for a specific type of classification,

1074
00:52:34,050 --> 00:52:35,550
whatever you define that class to be

1075
00:52:35,550 --> 00:52:39,990
is the extent to which the model can learn [INAUDIBLE].

1076
00:52:39,990 --> 00:52:42,842
So isn't this just a shortcoming of classification?

1077
00:52:42,842 --> 00:52:43,550
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

1078
00:52:43,550 --> 00:52:46,710
So the argument is, basically, this challenge

1079
00:52:46,710 --> 00:52:49,190
of being able to understand category boundaries

1080
00:52:49,190 --> 00:52:53,430
is maybe a limitation of the underlying philosophical concept

1081
00:52:53,430 --> 00:52:56,590
of trying to categorize things with some sort of list

1082
00:52:56,590 --> 00:52:58,350
of specific categories.

1083
00:52:58,350 --> 00:53:03,070
And actually, if you really want to get deep into it,

1084
00:53:03,070 --> 00:53:07,590
there's a lot of literature from philosophy about the concept

1085
00:53:07,590 --> 00:53:08,870
of categorization.

1086
00:53:08,870 --> 00:53:15,050
And there's kind of these explicit differences of opinion

1087
00:53:15,050 --> 00:53:17,510
when you look at historical philosophers around the idea

1088
00:53:17,510 --> 00:53:21,830
that it's possible to categorize something as a list of all

1089
00:53:21,830 --> 00:53:25,075
its attributes.

1090
00:53:25,075 --> 00:53:25,950
So you could say, OK.

1091
00:53:25,950 --> 00:53:28,850
A robin is a bird with a black face and a red chest

1092
00:53:28,850 --> 00:53:33,530
and yellow feet and a round structure

1093
00:53:33,530 --> 00:53:35,550
and a yellow eye with a black pupil.

1094
00:53:35,550 --> 00:53:39,690
And so there's this idea that any object can

1095
00:53:39,690 --> 00:53:43,170
be defined as a list of characteristics

1096
00:53:43,170 --> 00:53:45,370
you're looking for.

1097
00:53:45,370 --> 00:53:47,370
And then if you have the kind of mapping

1098
00:53:47,370 --> 00:53:50,190
of the set of characteristics to the things you care about,

1099
00:53:50,190 --> 00:53:52,410
then that's all you need.

1100
00:53:52,410 --> 00:53:53,970
My argument is kind of like, that's

1101
00:53:53,970 --> 00:53:56,090
the CLIP approach, in a way, because you're

1102
00:53:56,090 --> 00:53:58,090
trying to learn something about the relationship

1103
00:53:58,090 --> 00:54:01,742
between textual semantic concepts and things in images.

1104
00:54:01,742 --> 00:54:03,450
And there's been a lot of work since CLIP

1105
00:54:03,450 --> 00:54:07,170
that actually tries to really explicitly ground

1106
00:54:07,170 --> 00:54:10,970
semantic concepts from text into the visual components that

1107
00:54:10,970 --> 00:54:12,170
corresponds to.

1108
00:54:12,170 --> 00:54:17,130
But I think the argument is that, no matter what, if you

1109
00:54:17,130 --> 00:54:22,430
give me a list of a fixed list of attributes,

1110
00:54:22,430 --> 00:54:24,305
and you try to map that to a category,

1111
00:54:24,305 --> 00:54:26,930
you can basically come up with a counterexample that breaks it.

1112
00:54:30,115 --> 00:54:31,542
And then there's the question of,

1113
00:54:31,542 --> 00:54:33,250
where do you want to draw the boundaries?

1114
00:54:33,250 --> 00:54:37,110
That somehow has to be related to some goal.

1115
00:54:37,110 --> 00:54:39,450
So I'm getting really philosophical here.

1116
00:54:39,450 --> 00:54:46,750
So if I showed you like an apple and a bell pepper,

1117
00:54:46,750 --> 00:54:51,350
and there was a green apple and bell pepper and a red apple

1118
00:54:51,350 --> 00:54:55,430
and bell pepper, there's multiple reasonable ways

1119
00:54:55,430 --> 00:54:59,077
to split the set of groups.

1120
00:54:59,077 --> 00:55:00,910
So in the end, it always just comes down to,

1121
00:55:00,910 --> 00:55:04,190
what do you really need the representation space

1122
00:55:04,190 --> 00:55:05,330
to do for you?

1123
00:55:05,330 --> 00:55:07,590
And then what types of dimensions of variation

1124
00:55:07,590 --> 00:55:11,550
do you want to capture there?

1125
00:55:11,550 --> 00:55:16,670
And then the albino robin example is a great example

1126
00:55:16,670 --> 00:55:20,530
of ways that we are very good at generalizing,

1127
00:55:20,530 --> 00:55:24,090
but I would actually argue that models like CLIP still cannot

1128
00:55:24,090 --> 00:55:25,390
capture effectively.

1129
00:55:28,130 --> 00:55:28,630
Yeah.

1130
00:55:28,630 --> 00:55:31,270
And then you could tell me, if you were a bird expert,

1131
00:55:31,270 --> 00:55:33,830
oh, actually, that looks like it might be an albino robin,

1132
00:55:33,830 --> 00:55:35,455
but that's actually this other species.

1133
00:55:35,455 --> 00:55:39,270
And I'd believe you, because I don't know.

1134
00:55:39,270 --> 00:55:39,770
Yeah.

1135
00:55:39,770 --> 00:55:43,075
So I think this is really fascinating.

1136
00:55:43,075 --> 00:55:45,450
I think it starts getting at some really interesting kind

1137
00:55:45,450 --> 00:55:47,370
of meaty questions about, what do we really

1138
00:55:47,370 --> 00:55:50,130
want from representations?

1139
00:55:50,130 --> 00:55:53,210
And what does it really mean to move through a representation

1140
00:55:53,210 --> 00:55:54,490
space?

1141
00:55:54,490 --> 00:55:59,490
But so here, again, kind of going back to this idea of,

1142
00:55:59,490 --> 00:56:02,530
do contrastive learning from real data based

1143
00:56:02,530 --> 00:56:04,250
on some of heuristic set of rules

1144
00:56:04,250 --> 00:56:07,290
that we say don't break the class boundary versus if you do

1145
00:56:07,290 --> 00:56:10,610
it from generative data based on some experimental setup

1146
00:56:10,610 --> 00:56:15,250
of parameters around how far you can move in any given direction,

1147
00:56:15,250 --> 00:56:18,470
that you're able to now, instead of going from some data space

1148
00:56:18,470 --> 00:56:22,430
to a representation space, go from some latent space

1149
00:56:22,430 --> 00:56:25,630
into a data space into a representation space.

1150
00:56:25,630 --> 00:56:28,950
And they were able to show that if you're

1151
00:56:28,950 --> 00:56:31,990
doing linear transfer from ImageNet1000,

1152
00:56:31,990 --> 00:56:36,790
you can get 43.9% top-1 accuracy here.

1153
00:56:36,790 --> 00:56:43,190
But only using real data to train these models now mapping

1154
00:56:43,190 --> 00:56:44,698
through these kind of information

1155
00:56:44,698 --> 00:56:46,990
bottlenecks in a way of these different types of latent

1156
00:56:46,990 --> 00:56:48,810
spaces, you can do pretty well.

1157
00:56:48,810 --> 00:56:52,270
You can get 35.7% accuracy.

1158
00:56:52,270 --> 00:56:54,550
But then if you add in that ability

1159
00:56:54,550 --> 00:56:57,470
to move in that latent space, the accuracy

1160
00:56:57,470 --> 00:56:59,830
goes up to about 42.6%.

1161
00:56:59,830 --> 00:57:02,115
So here, you'll note neither of these

1162
00:57:02,115 --> 00:57:04,490
are as good as just training on the real data themselves.

1163
00:57:04,490 --> 00:57:06,270
So clearly, there's some limitations

1164
00:57:06,270 --> 00:57:07,830
in terms of the mechanism.

1165
00:57:07,830 --> 00:57:10,590
But the argument is that if you were

1166
00:57:10,590 --> 00:57:13,090
able to build the system effectively,

1167
00:57:13,090 --> 00:57:17,010
the ability to interpolate and have these reasonable dimensions

1168
00:57:17,010 --> 00:57:20,210
of variation within that original latent space

1169
00:57:20,210 --> 00:57:22,210
could potentially add a lot of value.

1170
00:57:22,210 --> 00:57:27,210
This is a pretty significant boost in performance.

1171
00:57:27,210 --> 00:57:29,930
So then the argument is that these deep generative views

1172
00:57:29,930 --> 00:57:33,650
is one dimension of variation, just different viewpoints,

1173
00:57:33,650 --> 00:57:37,010
can improve contrastive learning beyond only using standard data

1174
00:57:37,010 --> 00:57:37,950
augmentation.

1175
00:57:37,950 --> 00:57:40,850
And if the generative model is high quality,

1176
00:57:40,850 --> 00:57:42,450
you could even potentially outperform

1177
00:57:42,450 --> 00:57:44,033
learning from real data.

1178
00:57:44,033 --> 00:57:45,450
But that, of course, means that we

1179
00:57:45,450 --> 00:57:48,650
need the generator to be high quality, which

1180
00:57:48,650 --> 00:57:52,607
it's not for many things that are uncommon.

1181
00:57:52,607 --> 00:57:54,190
One of the things that is kind of fun,

1182
00:57:54,190 --> 00:57:56,110
if you try and play with these models,

1183
00:57:56,110 --> 00:57:58,970
is looking at what they understand

1184
00:57:58,970 --> 00:58:02,202
and trying to get them to break out of their priors.

1185
00:58:02,202 --> 00:58:03,910
So there's some classic examples of this.

1186
00:58:03,910 --> 00:58:07,990
If you ask models to generate a bunch of pictures of birds,

1187
00:58:07,990 --> 00:58:09,790
if you ask it to generate orioles,

1188
00:58:09,790 --> 00:58:11,790
it'll give you baseball players, right?

1189
00:58:11,790 --> 00:58:14,830
There's this semantic prior that gets

1190
00:58:14,830 --> 00:58:18,533
broken by the training data in really interesting ways.

1191
00:58:18,533 --> 00:58:20,450
But then there's also other interesting stuff.

1192
00:58:20,450 --> 00:58:25,030
If you ask the model to generate a toucan with a short beak,

1193
00:58:25,030 --> 00:58:27,110
it's really hard to get it to do it.

1194
00:58:27,110 --> 00:58:28,013
It just won't.

1195
00:58:28,013 --> 00:58:28,930
It's like, no, no, no.

1196
00:58:28,930 --> 00:58:33,310
Toucan somehow overpowers these mechanisms of morphology.

1197
00:58:33,310 --> 00:58:37,470
And I've also found that they're much better at generating

1198
00:58:37,470 --> 00:58:40,790
dimensions-- they're much better at changing texture than shape.

1199
00:58:40,790 --> 00:58:42,350
So it turns out that the models seem

1200
00:58:42,350 --> 00:58:44,630
to have learned really strong shape priors that

1201
00:58:44,630 --> 00:58:48,470
are hard to break out of for any given category,

1202
00:58:48,470 --> 00:58:51,630
but they've gotten quite good at swapping textures

1203
00:58:51,630 --> 00:58:52,370
for a category.

1204
00:58:52,370 --> 00:58:56,820
So you can say, oh, make a baseball, but make it furry.

1205
00:58:56,820 --> 00:58:57,570
And it's like, OK.

1206
00:58:57,570 --> 00:58:58,862
It swaps the texture just fine.

1207
00:58:58,862 --> 00:59:02,330
But if you say make a baseball, but make it star shaped,

1208
00:59:02,330 --> 00:59:03,907
it gets a little harder.

1209
00:59:03,907 --> 00:59:05,490
And I think that's really interesting.

1210
00:59:05,490 --> 00:59:06,865
I think it's interesting to think

1211
00:59:06,865 --> 00:59:10,970
about why shape might be encoded in a stronger way than texture.

1212
00:59:10,970 --> 00:59:14,650
And there's some recent work that shows some really nice--

1213
00:59:14,650 --> 00:59:17,250
in this space of trying to build effective

1214
00:59:17,250 --> 00:59:20,010
data augmentation through generation.

1215
00:59:20,010 --> 00:59:22,730
It just came out at ECCV.

1216
00:59:22,730 --> 00:59:30,110
They basically use the scene structure as a fixed thing.

1217
00:59:30,110 --> 00:59:32,305
So they mask out the entire dimension of the scene.

1218
00:59:32,305 --> 00:59:33,930
So now, you're guaranteeing that you're

1219
00:59:33,930 --> 00:59:36,263
going to have a realistic scene because it's a real one.

1220
00:59:36,263 --> 00:59:39,130
So maybe you have two dogs sitting on a bench.

1221
00:59:39,130 --> 00:59:42,130
Now, the way that they actually do their generation

1222
00:59:42,130 --> 00:59:47,690
to get these new cases is they take each individual object,

1223
00:59:47,690 --> 00:59:50,570
and then they change it to a different instance

1224
00:59:50,570 --> 00:59:52,010
within the data set.

1225
00:59:52,010 --> 00:59:54,850
So you basically say, I'm going to learn some personalization

1226
00:59:54,850 --> 00:59:58,250
model for every individual dog in the data set,

1227
00:59:58,250 --> 01:00:00,490
and then I'm going to condition on the mask

1228
01:00:00,490 --> 01:00:03,030
that I have and generate it with that instance.

1229
01:00:03,030 --> 01:00:06,290
So they basically call it instance augmented generation,

1230
01:00:06,290 --> 01:00:08,410
and they do get some really nice results.

1231
01:00:08,410 --> 01:00:10,870
And what it is, basically, it's breaking

1232
01:00:10,870 --> 01:00:13,910
these correlations between scene structure

1233
01:00:13,910 --> 01:00:16,990
and individual objects in a really realistic way.

1234
01:00:16,990 --> 01:00:20,990
And the images do look reasonably correct.

1235
01:00:20,990 --> 01:00:24,450
But the shape thing gets weird again.

1236
01:00:24,450 --> 01:00:29,270
So because that data set has bird defined as a category,

1237
01:00:29,270 --> 01:00:31,890
you can take any bird of any shape,

1238
01:00:31,890 --> 01:00:34,710
and you can replace it with the texture, essentially,

1239
01:00:34,710 --> 01:00:36,170
of a different bird.

1240
01:00:36,170 --> 01:00:37,890
But birds are very different shapes,

1241
01:00:37,890 --> 01:00:41,250
so it'll take something like a little round bird,

1242
01:00:41,250 --> 01:00:44,852
and then it'll try to squash an eagle into that shape.

1243
01:00:44,852 --> 01:00:46,310
And this is something that wouldn't

1244
01:00:46,310 --> 01:00:48,990
happen if the granularity of their categories

1245
01:00:48,990 --> 01:00:52,690
in the labeled space was a little bit more fine grained.

1246
01:00:52,690 --> 01:00:54,990
And again, it's just built in assumptions

1247
01:00:54,990 --> 01:00:56,790
about the amount of shape variation

1248
01:00:56,790 --> 01:00:59,590
you might have within a given defined category of interest

1249
01:00:59,590 --> 01:01:01,270
from a human perspective.

1250
01:01:01,270 --> 01:01:03,610
Anyway, that's enough philosophy for now.

1251
01:01:03,610 --> 01:01:05,670
So essentially, data plus plus says

1252
01:01:05,670 --> 01:01:09,970
that you might be able to sample from an implicit generative

1253
01:01:09,970 --> 01:01:12,410
model and use it to act like some

1254
01:01:12,410 --> 01:01:18,490
of decorative or interactive or mechanism to get data

1255
01:01:18,490 --> 01:01:20,050
with extra functionality.

1256
01:01:20,050 --> 01:01:22,070
And so you can define operators on that,

1257
01:01:22,070 --> 01:01:24,670
things like interpolation, extrapolation, manipulation,

1258
01:01:24,670 --> 01:01:26,890
composition, optimization, and you

1259
01:01:26,890 --> 01:01:30,650
can use it to label data potentially quite efficiently.

1260
01:01:30,650 --> 01:01:34,210
So the argument here is that maybe everything you

1261
01:01:34,210 --> 01:01:35,930
can do with regular data, you could also

1262
01:01:35,930 --> 01:01:40,330
try to do with this generation-augmented data.

1263
01:01:40,330 --> 01:01:41,630
And it might work better.

1264
01:01:41,630 --> 01:01:43,930
But I think there's still a lot of research that

1265
01:01:43,930 --> 01:01:48,610
needs to be done in this space to really achieve

1266
01:01:48,610 --> 01:01:51,170
the benefits that we think there's potential for.

1267
01:01:51,170 --> 01:01:53,270
We need generators that can learn with less data,

1268
01:01:53,270 --> 01:01:57,058
and we need to better understand how

1269
01:01:57,058 --> 01:01:58,350
these things are being learned.

1270
01:01:58,350 --> 01:01:58,930
Yeah.

1271
01:01:58,930 --> 01:02:00,055
AUDIENCE: So two questions.

1272
01:02:00,055 --> 01:02:01,030
One on the bias.

1273
01:02:01,030 --> 01:02:04,050
So I'm just curious to know, how can we figure out if there's

1274
01:02:04,050 --> 01:02:05,470
biases in the training?

1275
01:02:05,470 --> 01:02:10,110
Like in this case, we correlate darkness equals volcano eruption

1276
01:02:10,110 --> 01:02:12,470
without seeing the images, because this

1277
01:02:12,470 --> 01:02:15,110
is-- we can see it visually, but if the data set is

1278
01:02:15,110 --> 01:02:16,210
so large we can't see it.

1279
01:02:16,210 --> 01:02:18,630
And the second one is, how do we prioritize

1280
01:02:18,630 --> 01:02:20,890
diversity versus accuracy?

1281
01:02:20,890 --> 01:02:23,070
So the example you gave of a little bird

1282
01:02:23,070 --> 01:02:26,430
with the eagle features, but it's the size of little bird.

1283
01:02:26,430 --> 01:02:29,910
Is it great because it allows me to train for things

1284
01:02:29,910 --> 01:02:32,270
that aren't even possible, so maybe my testing data

1285
01:02:32,270 --> 01:02:33,150
will improve?

1286
01:02:33,150 --> 01:02:35,310
But it's so far away from fidelity.

1287
01:02:35,310 --> 01:02:37,630
Does that make this driven data bad?

1288
01:02:37,630 --> 01:02:38,470
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

1289
01:02:38,470 --> 01:02:40,820
So it's kind of like that question about the third eye

1290
01:02:40,820 --> 01:02:42,070
in the middle of the forehead.

1291
01:02:42,070 --> 01:02:43,903
If you start adding random eyes to your mom,

1292
01:02:43,903 --> 01:02:45,630
and it makes the model more robust

1293
01:02:45,630 --> 01:02:51,030
at recognizing your mom in real data, is that a problem?

1294
01:02:51,030 --> 01:02:53,510
So with relation to the first thing,

1295
01:02:53,510 --> 01:02:55,430
for example, that data set interfaces paper

1296
01:02:55,430 --> 01:02:58,070
was a mechanism that tries to use the fact that you can

1297
01:02:58,070 --> 01:03:01,070
interact with these systems with text

1298
01:03:01,070 --> 01:03:03,687
to probe biases efficiently without needing to explicitly

1299
01:03:03,687 --> 01:03:04,770
look through all the data.

1300
01:03:04,770 --> 01:03:06,562
So I think there's ways people have thought

1301
01:03:06,562 --> 01:03:08,250
of using generative models to help

1302
01:03:08,250 --> 01:03:10,330
you find biases efficiently.

1303
01:03:10,330 --> 01:03:12,650
And then with respect to the second one,

1304
01:03:12,650 --> 01:03:14,350
I think it basically depends.

1305
01:03:18,410 --> 01:03:21,930
There's some work that tries to demonstrate

1306
01:03:21,930 --> 01:03:24,170
that even for really fine grained things like bird

1307
01:03:24,170 --> 01:03:28,290
species, you can learn to generate data that

1308
01:03:28,290 --> 01:03:30,290
is accurately of that bird species

1309
01:03:30,290 --> 01:03:37,343
by using a classification model as guidance for the generator.

1310
01:03:37,343 --> 01:03:39,010
But essentially, what that means is then

1311
01:03:39,010 --> 01:03:40,690
you're building a generator that's

1312
01:03:40,690 --> 01:03:43,275
optimized for a given classification model,

1313
01:03:43,275 --> 01:03:45,150
and you can take that to its logical extreme,

1314
01:03:45,150 --> 01:03:47,330
because it essentially means you can map out

1315
01:03:47,330 --> 01:03:50,650
the boundaries of that classification model really

1316
01:03:50,650 --> 01:03:54,270
explicitly moving into spaces that aren't super realistic.

1317
01:03:54,270 --> 01:03:57,850
And then you can maybe get better performance

1318
01:03:57,850 --> 01:04:03,290
for a given manifold or a given representation space.

1319
01:04:03,290 --> 01:04:07,350
But it might not always correspond to things you want.

1320
01:04:07,350 --> 01:04:10,710
So for example, you get better performance on crows

1321
01:04:10,710 --> 01:04:14,190
when you add in images where it's like Bigfoot with a crow

1322
01:04:14,190 --> 01:04:15,070
texture.

1323
01:04:15,070 --> 01:04:17,830
And it kind of gets to this weird question of,

1324
01:04:17,830 --> 01:04:20,647
we're getting better results on our test data,

1325
01:04:20,647 --> 01:04:22,230
but is it actually going to correspond

1326
01:04:22,230 --> 01:04:26,230
to better results in practice beyond this

1327
01:04:26,230 --> 01:04:28,030
of fixed set of test data?

1328
01:04:28,030 --> 01:04:31,210
And I think that gets kind of hard to quantify.

1329
01:04:31,210 --> 01:04:35,050
So we're good at optimizing for metrics on test data sets,

1330
01:04:35,050 --> 01:04:38,390
but I think it's much harder if you're moving to the real world.

1331
01:04:38,390 --> 01:04:41,190
You could imagine it might actually be better in some ways

1332
01:04:41,190 --> 01:04:48,470
to have things that aren't, I guess, different in ways that--

1333
01:04:48,470 --> 01:04:50,410
weird and not realistic.

1334
01:04:50,410 --> 01:04:52,598
Because maybe in the real world, you

1335
01:04:52,598 --> 01:04:54,390
might be more likely to encounter something

1336
01:04:54,390 --> 01:04:57,170
that actually does look a little bit like a small eagle,

1337
01:04:57,170 --> 01:04:59,430
but it's actually something else entirely.

1338
01:04:59,430 --> 01:05:02,650
So maybe the open endedness of the real world

1339
01:05:02,650 --> 01:05:06,210
makes the question of maximizing the potential

1340
01:05:06,210 --> 01:05:08,290
within a kind of a fixed, closed world

1341
01:05:08,290 --> 01:05:12,050
setting a little bit less translatable.

1342
01:05:12,050 --> 01:05:12,550
But yeah.

1343
01:05:12,550 --> 01:05:14,258
I don't know if that kind of makes sense.

1344
01:05:14,258 --> 01:05:15,130
I'm sort of ranting.

1345
01:05:15,130 --> 01:05:18,890
But I do think that what is useful

1346
01:05:18,890 --> 01:05:22,010
is going to be directly related to your task.

1347
01:05:22,010 --> 01:05:25,230
So for different types of tasks, for different goals,

1348
01:05:25,230 --> 01:05:28,450
useful data will be different.

1349
01:05:28,450 --> 01:05:29,650
Yeah.

1350
01:05:29,650 --> 01:05:31,850
AUDIENCE: Is image domain the bulk

1351
01:05:31,850 --> 01:05:34,330
of where the cutting edge research is

1352
01:05:34,330 --> 01:05:39,098
being done for this topic, or is that a pedagogical reason?

1353
01:05:39,098 --> 01:05:41,390
SARA BEERY: Why am I showing a bunch of image examples?

1354
01:05:41,390 --> 01:05:42,410
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

1355
01:05:42,410 --> 01:05:45,910
It would probably be harder to do these evaluations on text

1356
01:05:45,910 --> 01:05:47,690
because you'd have to each read each one

1357
01:05:47,690 --> 01:05:49,410
and see the differences.

1358
01:05:49,410 --> 01:05:50,290
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

1359
01:05:50,290 --> 01:05:51,370
That's interesting.

1360
01:05:51,370 --> 01:05:54,810
So there definitely is research on generative text

1361
01:05:54,810 --> 01:05:57,330
as data augmentation, but I think

1362
01:05:57,330 --> 01:05:59,290
you're right that it's kind of harder--

1363
01:05:59,290 --> 01:06:03,910
class boundaries and text is a little bit harder to define.

1364
01:06:03,910 --> 01:06:08,730
And also, I could imagine if you think about generative songs,

1365
01:06:08,730 --> 01:06:13,590
for example, maybe part of it is just the nuanced, messy space

1366
01:06:13,590 --> 01:06:16,310
of what a category is.

1367
01:06:16,310 --> 01:06:19,270
But there definitely is work on both generating data

1368
01:06:19,270 --> 01:06:22,030
and using generative data for training in the tech space

1369
01:06:22,030 --> 01:06:26,470
and in the audio space and in many other modalities as well.

1370
01:06:26,470 --> 01:06:27,570
Lots of work.

1371
01:06:27,570 --> 01:06:29,350
Actually, classical work that used

1372
01:06:29,350 --> 01:06:32,190
generated data for training in medical imagery,

1373
01:06:32,190 --> 01:06:34,670
because often, we had so few real data

1374
01:06:34,670 --> 01:06:37,830
that really carefully handcrafted synthesizers of data

1375
01:06:37,830 --> 01:06:39,630
were quite effective.

1376
01:06:39,630 --> 01:06:42,830
There is kind of a much broader kind of interesting debate

1377
01:06:42,830 --> 01:06:47,030
currently about what's called model collapse

1378
01:06:47,030 --> 01:06:49,530
or manifold collapse where, essentially,

1379
01:06:49,530 --> 01:06:53,390
if you start training a model on more data that's generated

1380
01:06:53,390 --> 01:06:55,990
than data that's real-- which is a very clear concern

1381
01:06:55,990 --> 01:06:58,890
today, because more and more of the text on the internet

1382
01:06:58,890 --> 01:07:01,490
is generated text as opposed to real text--

1383
01:07:01,490 --> 01:07:04,330
there's an entire line of research that essentially shows

1384
01:07:04,330 --> 01:07:10,610
that once you go too far down the ratio of more

1385
01:07:10,610 --> 01:07:13,210
of the data being generated than real,

1386
01:07:13,210 --> 01:07:14,750
it becomes kind of a vicious cycle.

1387
01:07:14,750 --> 01:07:16,930
And you can have these types of model collapse

1388
01:07:16,930 --> 01:07:19,770
where you start to lose parts of your distribution

1389
01:07:19,770 --> 01:07:22,128
that were captured in the real data because

1390
01:07:22,128 --> 01:07:24,170
of the kind of distribution of the generated data

1391
01:07:24,170 --> 01:07:25,585
based on the likelihood.

1392
01:07:25,585 --> 01:07:28,210
And there's fascinating-- it's a pretty active area of research

1393
01:07:28,210 --> 01:07:29,730
right now, this question of, what

1394
01:07:29,730 --> 01:07:33,970
does it look like to have models trained from generated data?

1395
01:07:33,970 --> 01:07:37,830
And what are the kind of failure modes that can result in?

1396
01:07:37,830 --> 01:07:38,330
Cool.

1397
01:07:38,330 --> 01:07:40,610
So in the last little bit, I'm going

1398
01:07:40,610 --> 01:07:43,810
to talk a little bit about learning to learn quickly.

1399
01:07:43,810 --> 01:07:46,953
So this idea of not just, what is a mechanism

1400
01:07:46,953 --> 01:07:47,870
for transfer learning?

1401
01:07:47,870 --> 01:07:50,690
But actually, how do you learn a model that

1402
01:07:50,690 --> 01:07:55,050
is good at transfer learning, a model that adapts quickly?

1403
01:07:55,050 --> 01:07:58,550
So here, the idea is that maybe instead

1404
01:07:58,550 --> 01:08:00,430
of just taking the classical approach where

1405
01:08:00,430 --> 01:08:03,030
you have learning, which is this middle part where you have

1406
01:08:03,030 --> 01:08:05,650
a data set, a learner, you get some parameters,

1407
01:08:05,650 --> 01:08:07,778
and then at inference time, you take your data set,

1408
01:08:07,778 --> 01:08:09,570
you run it through a model with parameters,

1409
01:08:09,570 --> 01:08:13,950
and you get predictions, you add this meta layer on top

1410
01:08:13,950 --> 01:08:15,890
of taking a bunch of different data sets

1411
01:08:15,890 --> 01:08:17,590
and training some of meta learner

1412
01:08:17,590 --> 01:08:19,910
that has a different set of hyperparameters

1413
01:08:19,910 --> 01:08:25,390
that then you transfer to any given learner and data set.

1414
01:08:25,390 --> 01:08:28,069
And so one of the seminal works in this space

1415
01:08:28,069 --> 01:08:30,990
was this work called MAML by Chelsea Finn--

1416
01:08:30,990 --> 01:08:34,710
stands for model-agnostic meta-learning.

1417
01:08:34,710 --> 01:08:36,630
And so far, we saw transfer learning as,

1418
01:08:36,630 --> 01:08:39,250
given some prior model and representation,

1419
01:08:39,250 --> 01:08:41,510
how can we quickly adapt it?

1420
01:08:41,510 --> 01:08:44,010
And this was the first work that tried to ask,

1421
01:08:44,010 --> 01:08:47,710
could we learn to do transfer learning effectively?

1422
01:08:47,710 --> 01:08:50,149
So now, consider you're doing fine tuning.

1423
01:08:50,149 --> 01:08:53,710
Given some initialization we, update it with SGD.

1424
01:08:53,710 --> 01:08:56,930
MAML tries to learn an initialization such

1425
01:08:56,930 --> 01:08:59,689
that it's easily adaptable with just a few steps

1426
01:08:59,689 --> 01:09:02,090
of stochastic gradient descent.

1427
01:09:02,090 --> 01:09:04,890
And so if you look at this algorithmically,

1428
01:09:04,890 --> 01:09:09,890
essentially, you now are doing like a double loop

1429
01:09:09,890 --> 01:09:11,490
in your optimization.

1430
01:09:11,490 --> 01:09:14,090
So starting with your initialization,

1431
01:09:14,090 --> 01:09:18,870
for any given data set that you're going to train over,

1432
01:09:18,870 --> 01:09:21,569
you now run a few steps of SGD and then compute loss

1433
01:09:21,569 --> 01:09:23,130
after those steps.

1434
01:09:23,130 --> 01:09:26,010
And then you backprop through that back

1435
01:09:26,010 --> 01:09:30,850
to your initial meta-learning initial set of parameters.

1436
01:09:30,850 --> 01:09:32,930
So how do you actually backprop through something

1437
01:09:32,930 --> 01:09:38,130
that's now like an inner loop within your larger loop?

1438
01:09:38,130 --> 01:09:41,689
So if you're doing one step of stochastic gradient descent,

1439
01:09:41,689 --> 01:09:45,770
you essentially can consider that a forwards pass

1440
01:09:45,770 --> 01:09:49,450
over x plus the backwards of the forwards of x and theta

1441
01:09:49,450 --> 01:09:50,130
plus theta.

1442
01:09:50,130 --> 01:09:54,530
Essentially, remember when we took a single part of a model,

1443
01:09:54,530 --> 01:09:57,090
and then we showed that if you wanted to do back propagation,

1444
01:09:57,090 --> 01:09:59,910
you could basically unfold it, and it was essentially

1445
01:09:59,910 --> 01:10:03,430
one big forward pass of going through forward

1446
01:10:03,430 --> 01:10:06,270
and then backward of a model?

1447
01:10:06,270 --> 01:10:09,630
So now if you want to do two steps of stochastic gradient

1448
01:10:09,630 --> 01:10:12,830
descent, again, you're kind of nesting this.

1449
01:10:12,830 --> 01:10:15,630
And so essentially, this is saying that you

1450
01:10:15,630 --> 01:10:19,910
can backprop through backward.

1451
01:10:19,910 --> 01:10:21,667
So if you're going forward and backward

1452
01:10:21,667 --> 01:10:22,750
through a simple network--

1453
01:10:22,750 --> 01:10:24,910
I don't know if you guys remember this.

1454
01:10:24,910 --> 01:10:27,513
So here's that forward pass.

1455
01:10:27,513 --> 01:10:28,930
Then, you have your backward pass.

1456
01:10:28,930 --> 01:10:31,290
And then here, this is where we kind of unfold it.

1457
01:10:31,290 --> 01:10:36,350
And so now, you can show that doing forward and backward

1458
01:10:36,350 --> 01:10:38,870
through a simple network is just one forward pass.

1459
01:10:38,870 --> 01:10:44,030
And then you can do this meta backward through everything.

1460
01:10:44,030 --> 01:10:46,430
So it's possible.

1461
01:10:46,430 --> 01:10:48,630
It's just expensive, essentially,

1462
01:10:48,630 --> 01:10:51,170
is the main point here, because you're basically

1463
01:10:51,170 --> 01:10:55,038
taking those nested loops of forward and backward,

1464
01:10:55,038 --> 01:10:56,830
and you're just stretching all of them out.

1465
01:10:56,830 --> 01:11:00,890
And you're now getting what is a very, very deep network

1466
01:11:00,890 --> 01:11:04,130
that you're going to optimize through.

1467
01:11:04,130 --> 01:11:08,570
Now, this type of computational complexity

1468
01:11:08,570 --> 01:11:10,730
often means that the relative benefit

1469
01:11:10,730 --> 01:11:13,610
of these types of meta-learning models-- though nice in theory,

1470
01:11:13,610 --> 01:11:16,712
and you get really nice gains on maybe simple problems--

1471
01:11:16,712 --> 01:11:18,170
it's often just too computationally

1472
01:11:18,170 --> 01:11:21,770
expensive to really deploy.

1473
01:11:21,770 --> 01:11:26,770
But there are a few examples of how people have actually

1474
01:11:26,770 --> 01:11:29,170
used these types of meta-learning

1475
01:11:29,170 --> 01:11:31,490
for real world applications.

1476
01:11:31,490 --> 01:11:33,462
So in this case, in practice, I was

1477
01:11:33,462 --> 01:11:35,170
saying the gains of the method, something

1478
01:11:35,170 --> 01:11:36,590
like model-agnostic meta-learning,

1479
01:11:36,590 --> 01:11:38,530
are often not worth the additional computational cost

1480
01:11:38,530 --> 01:11:40,210
at training time, and sometimes can

1481
01:11:40,210 --> 01:11:43,370
be worse than just like pretraining on all of those

1482
01:11:43,370 --> 01:11:46,530
different data sets and then fine tuning.

1483
01:11:46,530 --> 01:11:51,170
But Gabi Tseng introduced this geospatial embedding,

1484
01:11:51,170 --> 01:11:55,190
essentially like a bit of a task-specific tokenizer,

1485
01:11:55,190 --> 01:11:57,630
within that meta-learning objective

1486
01:11:57,630 --> 01:12:00,890
that encoded some kind of task-specific context.

1487
01:12:00,890 --> 01:12:04,470
And they showed that giving this task specificity

1488
01:12:04,470 --> 01:12:06,590
within that meta-learning loop did give them

1489
01:12:06,590 --> 01:12:09,910
some really nice gains when they were looking

1490
01:12:09,910 --> 01:12:12,270
at crop type categorization for corn

1491
01:12:12,270 --> 01:12:14,110
and a bunch of different countries.

1492
01:12:14,110 --> 01:12:16,510
So here, these are a comparison of a bunch

1493
01:12:16,510 --> 01:12:17,890
of different types of methods.

1494
01:12:17,890 --> 01:12:19,710
But you can see at the top is kind

1495
01:12:19,710 --> 01:12:24,570
of versus meta-learning or just pretraining a model, there,

1496
01:12:24,570 --> 01:12:25,970
it's about the same.

1497
01:12:25,970 --> 01:12:29,150
But this task conditioning that gives your model the ability

1498
01:12:29,150 --> 01:12:31,910
to specialize effectively, maybe to a given country or part

1499
01:12:31,910 --> 01:12:34,847
of the world, ends up being pretty effective.

1500
01:12:34,847 --> 01:12:36,930
This is maybe-- I don't know if you guys remember,

1501
01:12:36,930 --> 01:12:38,710
but we talked a bit about this idea

1502
01:12:38,710 --> 01:12:40,670
of maybe learning a prior when we were talking

1503
01:12:40,670 --> 01:12:42,430
about distribution shift.

1504
01:12:42,430 --> 01:12:44,070
This is maybe a different mechanism

1505
01:12:44,070 --> 01:12:46,750
of trying to learn something like a geospatial prior

1506
01:12:46,750 --> 01:12:49,730
or some of useful contextualization

1507
01:12:49,730 --> 01:12:53,890
so that your model can actually build some knowledge about how

1508
01:12:53,890 --> 01:12:56,210
it should adapt for specific types of problems

1509
01:12:56,210 --> 01:12:59,290
or types of tasks.

1510
01:12:59,290 --> 01:13:00,890
And so there's also this question

1511
01:13:00,890 --> 01:13:03,810
of meta-learning by sequence modeling.

1512
01:13:03,810 --> 01:13:06,290
So definition of learning.

1513
01:13:06,290 --> 01:13:09,810
Maybe we have a sequence of input

1514
01:13:09,810 --> 01:13:12,170
and ground truth, input and ground truth

1515
01:13:12,170 --> 01:13:13,610
for a bunch of things.

1516
01:13:13,610 --> 01:13:16,050
And then you output some function such

1517
01:13:16,050 --> 01:13:18,610
that if you give it a new input, it'll

1518
01:13:18,610 --> 01:13:20,980
give you a new ground truth.

1519
01:13:20,980 --> 01:13:22,730
So maybe this is kind of like that example

1520
01:13:22,730 --> 01:13:23,930
we talked about when we were talking

1521
01:13:23,930 --> 01:13:26,410
about autoregressive modeling for large language models

1522
01:13:26,410 --> 01:13:28,010
where we could say, OK.

1523
01:13:28,010 --> 01:13:30,050
We're going to show you an example of English

1524
01:13:30,050 --> 01:13:31,770
and the translation in Spanish.

1525
01:13:31,770 --> 01:13:33,230
I'm going to show you another example in English,

1526
01:13:33,230 --> 01:13:34,830
another example of the translation in Spanish.

1527
01:13:34,830 --> 01:13:36,497
Now, if I show you something in English,

1528
01:13:36,497 --> 01:13:38,650
the model would automatically generate

1529
01:13:38,650 --> 01:13:40,510
the translation in Spanish.

1530
01:13:40,510 --> 01:13:44,650
This idea of sequence based in-context learning.

1531
01:13:44,650 --> 01:13:46,710
And so here, if you have something

1532
01:13:46,710 --> 01:13:48,830
like a recurrent neural network, the state

1533
01:13:48,830 --> 01:13:51,900
plus the parameters of that recurrent neural network,

1534
01:13:51,900 --> 01:13:53,650
or something like an autoregressive model,

1535
01:13:53,650 --> 01:13:58,050
like predicting the next word, is f, that current state.

1536
01:13:58,050 --> 01:14:00,370
And then you apply that f to your new input.

1537
01:14:00,370 --> 01:14:03,430
You're basically saying, predict the next thing.

1538
01:14:03,430 --> 01:14:05,710
And so then the hidden state of that RNN

1539
01:14:05,710 --> 01:14:09,670
or that recurrent neural network is a model that arguably

1540
01:14:09,670 --> 01:14:11,230
learns to learn, right?

1541
01:14:11,230 --> 01:14:13,590
It takes some statistics of the sequence in general

1542
01:14:13,590 --> 01:14:17,070
that enable it to predict what should come next.

1543
01:14:17,070 --> 01:14:19,270
And this really is, I think, what

1544
01:14:19,270 --> 01:14:23,470
GPT and a lot of these modern autoregressive models are doing.

1545
01:14:23,470 --> 01:14:25,770
It's kind of a version of meta-learning at large scale.

1546
01:14:25,770 --> 01:14:27,603
And you could argue that in-context learning

1547
01:14:27,603 --> 01:14:30,950
is kind of just another mechanism of meta-learning.

1548
01:14:30,950 --> 01:14:33,410
Because when these models are being trained,

1549
01:14:33,410 --> 01:14:36,470
you're showing them a lot of different possible tasks

1550
01:14:36,470 --> 01:14:38,710
or sequences and then asking them

1551
01:14:38,710 --> 01:14:43,330
to learn how to really quickly and efficiently generate

1552
01:14:43,330 --> 01:14:44,610
answers.

1553
01:14:44,610 --> 01:14:47,090
What comes next for new sequences?

1554
01:14:47,090 --> 01:14:52,850
So you could argue that there's a pretty similar motivation

1555
01:14:52,850 --> 01:14:57,010
and potentially a much better actual output when you think

1556
01:14:57,010 --> 01:15:00,655
about meta-learning through sequence modeling

1557
01:15:00,655 --> 01:15:02,530
as opposed to meta-learning through this kind

